WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.244
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4488.

2
00:00:01.244 --> 00:00:02.488
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.488 --> 00:00:05.805
<v REBECCA COOPER>Okay great, so today is July twenty-seventh, 2015.

4
00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:13.683
I am Becca Cooper representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:13.683 --> 00:00:21.146
Today we are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

6
00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:27.780
I'm here today with Ms. Della Dotson at her home in Moss Bluff [Calcasieu Parish, Louisiana].

7
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.268
Thank for agreeing to be interviewed.

8
00:00:30.268 --> 00:00:31.098
<v DELLA DOTSON>You’re welcome.

9
00:00:31.098 --> 00:00:34.000
<v COOPER>This is our first interview together.

10
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.556
<v DOTSON>Thanks, that's good.

11
00:00:35.556 --> 00:00:41.259
<v COOPER>So, could you please state your full name for the recording?

12
00:00:41.259 --> 00:00:43.333
<v DOTSON>Della Ann Rigmaiden Dotson.

13
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:43.852
<v COOPER>Awesome.

14
00:00:43.852 --> 00:00:46.963
And what year were you born?

15
00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:48.000
<v DOTSON>1948.

16
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.000
<v COOPER>Where were you born?

17
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.000
<v DOTSON>Mossville, Louisiana.

18
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.500
<v COOPER>What street did you first live on?

19
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.000
<v DOTSON>It was Old Spanish Trail.

20
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.000
<v COOPER>Wonderful, and do you have any siblings?

21
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:11.857
<v DOTSON>Yes, I have one whole sister, and the rest of them are all deceased.

22
00:01:11.857 --> 00:01:16.510
They were half brothers and sisters.

23
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.612
<v COOPER>What were their names?

24
00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:26.592
<v DOTSON>Wesley Rigmaiden, Carl Rigmaiden, Christine Davis, and Denise Rigmaiden.

25
00:01:26.592 --> 00:01:30.469
<v COOPER>And your full sister's name?

26
00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:33.571
<v DOTSON>Lola Bell Rigmaiden James.

27
00:01:33.571 --> 00:01:39.000
<v COOPER>And what are your parents' names?

28
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.939
<v DOTSON>Joshua Rigmaiden and Norine Smith

29
00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:44.877
<v COOPER>Smith is her maiden name?

30
00:01:44.877 --> 00:01:48.404
<v DOTSON>No, Fowler is her maiden name.

31
00:01:48.404 --> 00:01:58.395
Her . . . She was married to my dad which her name was Rigmaiden and after my dad died,

32
00:01:58.395 --> 00:02:03.684
she remarried and that's where the Smith comes from.

33
00:02:03.684 --> 00:02:04.860
<v COOPER>I see.

34
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:05.447
Okay.

35
00:02:05.447 --> 00:02:11.325
You also mentioned earlier that your father was remarried, too?

36
00:02:11.325 --> 00:02:21.904
<v DOTSON>Yes, well, the first wife I don't know her name, and Mother L was one of his wives.

37
00:02:21.904 --> 00:02:32.482
Mother L was the first wife, and my mother was the second wife, and her name was Norine.

38
00:02:32.482 --> 00:02:39.535
And, it was two children born to that marriage with my mom.

39
00:02:39.535 --> 00:02:48.351
And the other four, CB [Carl] and Wesley were whole brothers, and Christine and Denise,

40
00:02:48.351 --> 00:02:55.404
I'm not sure about them because they were much older than us.

41
00:02:55.404 --> 00:02:57.754
So, I don't know.

42
00:02:57.754 --> 00:03:08.333
Now Carolyn, when you interview Carolyn Martin, she should be able to bring you up to that point.

43
00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:08.921
<v COOPER>Okay.

44
00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:15.974
Yeah, we will definitely make a note to ask her about that.

45
00:03:15.974 --> 00:03:19.500
So, where were your parents from?

46
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.439
Where was your mom from?

47
00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:26.553
<v DOTSON>My mother was from Indian Hills, Texas.

48
00:03:26.553 --> 00:03:27.140
<v COOPER>Okay.

49
00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:30.667
<v DOTSON>And my father was from Mossville.

50
00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:42.421
Well they . . . When his parents originally came, they settled on the West Fork which is the . . . West Fork is like,

51
00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:48.886
almost in Houston River area, but that's still in Calcasieu Parish.

52
00:03:48.886 --> 00:03:53.000
Then, they came on to Mossville.

53
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.702
<v COOPER>Do you remember the year that your father's family . . . Or around what time?

54
00:04:00.702 --> 00:04:04.850
<v DOTSON>No, I don't know the time period.

55
00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:11.367
<v COOPER>Do you happen to remember why they first came to Mossville?

56
00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:14.329
Or to the Calcasieu Parish?

57
00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:24.994
<v DOTSON>They were . . . My grandparents were into the lumber [business] on the Calcasieu [River] and at the West Fork,

58
00:04:24.994 --> 00:04:34.474
they [coughs] I guess cut down trees and made lumber out of the trees and stuff.

59
00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:46.916
And I think the name of the company, I think . . . I'm not sure if it was Powell’s lumber company or what,

60
00:04:46.916 --> 00:04:53.434
but some of the other siblings would know . . . doing Carolyn’s interview.

61
00:04:53.434 --> 00:05:04.098
Now Carl Ray, which is . . . We call him CB, that's Carl Rigmaiden, that's my brother, my half-brother's son.

62
00:05:04.098 --> 00:05:11.801
He might can bring you up to that, in the interview with Carolyn.

63
00:05:11.801 --> 00:05:21.873
Because they're older than I am, so they'll go farther back than I can on that period.

64
00:05:21.873 --> 00:05:29.575
<v COOPER>And your mother moved to Mossville from Texas, do you know what time?

65
00:05:29.575 --> 00:05:38.462
<v DOTSON>My daddy was a minister, and . . . he had. . . My daddy was like a building minister.

66
00:05:38.462 --> 00:05:47.350
He was . . . let's see how to say that . . . Well to back up a little bit,

67
00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:57.422
Mother L and my father . . . Mother L was . . . The Christ Sanctified Holy Religion was founded by them,

68
00:05:57.422 --> 00:06:07.494
and my dad was the president of the district and he was considered as a building minister.

69
00:06:07.494 --> 00:06:27.046
And they would send him to different areas that needed a church built and he would stay there long enough to establish the churches and that's how he met my mom in Texas,

70
00:06:27.046 --> 00:06:37.711
because where they were from, it was so rural . . . oh it was . . . Nothing but red clay and sand,

71
00:06:37.711 --> 00:06:41.266
but I don't know what year.

72
00:06:41.266 --> 00:06:51.338
But I know my mom was a lot younger than my dad and he brought her back.

73
00:06:51.338 --> 00:07:00.225
They married here I believe, and they . . . well my daddy was . . . They settled in Mossville.

74
00:07:00.225 --> 00:07:10.298
And my daddy had . . . The house that my daddy built for us was a two story apartment,

75
00:07:10.298 --> 00:07:18.000
that's where I was born, and . . . my sister was born in Texas.

76
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:29.393
My mother went back home [Texas] to have Lola, but I was born here [Mossville] by Doctor Ross.

77
00:07:29.393 --> 00:07:35.723
And it was January first, 1948 when I was born,

78
00:07:35.723 --> 00:07:49.648
and the place that I was born where we just had it demolitioned [demolished] when Sasol [energy and chemical company] bought out,

79
00:07:49.648 --> 00:07:59.776
and, my dad built another house on the side where we just had that demoed [demolished].

80
00:07:59.776 --> 00:08:06.105
My sister was living in that until they demoed it.

81
00:08:06.105 --> 00:08:16.233
And the . . . where I was born after my daddy died, my mom . . . It was an apartment,

82
00:08:16.233 --> 00:08:30.158
well it was our house that used to be on the top, and then my mom added three more apartments onto that.

83
00:08:30.158 --> 00:08:48.514
And I think, I'm not positive but I think we might have had the first rent houses in Mossville because everybody you really talked to stayed in those apartments.

84
00:08:48.514 --> 00:09:03.705
They moved in, moved out till they got something built, and it was right at the end of Prater Road and Old Spanish Trail.

85
00:09:03.705 --> 00:09:10.035
Right . . . You come across Prater and that's where it was.

86
00:09:10.035 --> 00:09:25.226
And now there was a pond that was dug in the back off the apartments, where my daddy gave dirt to build Prater Road.

87
00:09:25.226 --> 00:09:34.088
<c.p>A lot of more families had moved in by then, and by that time,

88
00:09:34.088 --> 00:09:41.051
I think the . . . Well we were in the original five families.

89
00:09:41.051 --> 00:09:53.710
The Rigmaidens were in the first original families, the first five, and after that I think it went to fifteen,

90
00:09:53.710 --> 00:10:00.673
and all . . . Like, I don't know what else after that point.

91
00:10:00.673 --> 00:10:09.534
But, now as time went on, we went . . . I went to Mossville Elementary School,

92
00:10:09.534 --> 00:10:16.497
everybody went . . . There was where the Recreation Center is right now,

93
00:10:16.497 --> 00:10:27.890
Mossville School used to be there, and it was one, two, I think it was three buildings there.

94
00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:41.182
One was the cafeteria, one was the main building, and I think the other one was for like the upper grades.

95
00:10:41.182 --> 00:10:48.778
And most of the children . . . See Mossville didn't have a high school.

96
00:10:48.778 --> 00:11:04.602
And I know during the time that my sister got to high school, they sent them to Washington High School, which is in Lake Charles.

97
00:11:04.602 --> 00:11:14.097
There was a bus that would come from Vinton, Louisiana, would stop in Sulphur [Louisiana],

98
00:11:14.097 --> 00:11:25.490
pick up all the high school kids in Sulphur, pick up all the high school kids in Mossville,

99
00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:31.820
pick up all of the high school kids in Westlake,

100
00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:45.112
and they would be transported to Washington High School because there was no black high school for them in that area.

101
00:11:45.112 --> 00:11:52.075
And a lot of them graduated [from Washington High] . . . well no,

102
00:11:52.075 --> 00:11:58.404
some of them went to . . . They came back to Mossville,

103
00:11:58.404 --> 00:12:06.000
Mossville High School was built in nineteen . . . let's see that's . . . 1955.

104
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:14.750
<v COOPER>Oh, yeah, do you want to explain what you're looking at for the recording?

105
00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:31.625
<v DOTSON>That's a rug that we had made of Mossville because the name of the school was Mossville Pirates and the year the school was built was 1955,

106
00:12:31.625 --> 00:12:34.750
and it closed in 1991.

107
00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:35.375
<v COOPER>Wow.

108
00:12:35.375 --> 00:12:47.250
<v DOTSON>And my daddy was very instrumental in getting the high school built in Mossville, and also the recreation center.

109
00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.750
<v COOPER>The Rigmaiden Recreation Center . . .

110
00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:51.000
<v DOTSON>Yes.

111
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:57.389
<v COOPER>I was going to ask you if that was named after one of your relatives.

112
00:12:57.389 --> 00:13:01.648
<v DOTSON>The complex, the whole thing, is after my daddy and . . .

113
00:13:01.648 --> 00:13:02.500
<v COOPER>Joshua Rigmaiden?

114
00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:03.352
<v DOTSON>Joshua Rigmaiden.

115
00:13:03.352 --> 00:13:07.611
<v COOPER>Wow, so he did a lot, he was very instrumental . . .

116
00:13:07.611 --> 00:13:11.444
<v DOTSON>He was . . . They considered him the mayor of Mossville.

117
00:13:11.444 --> 00:13:13.148
<v COOPER>The mayor of Mossville.

118
00:13:13.148 --> 00:13:14.000
<v DOTSON>Yes.

119
00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:14.828
<v COOPER>That's amazing.

120
00:13:14.828 --> 00:13:23.936
So I have a few questions, you just gave us so much, and just to go back to get some clarification so,

121
00:13:23.936 --> 00:13:30.975
the . . . You said that your family maybe was the first to rent in Mossville on Prater Road,

122
00:13:30.975 --> 00:13:35.115
was your family the first to build on Prater Road?

123
00:13:35.115 --> 00:13:40.911
<v DOTSON>No, it was . . . Prater Road is like this, Old Spanish Trail is like this,

124
00:13:40.911 --> 00:13:46.707
and if you come across Prater Road is where our property was, right there.

125
00:13:46.707 --> 00:13:53.331
<v COOPER>Oh my goodness, and were those buildings that he built, were those associated with the ministry?

126
00:13:53.331 --> 00:13:53.745
<v DOTSON>No.

127
00:13:53.745 --> 00:13:55.401
<v COOPER>No, they were just . . .

128
00:13:55.401 --> 00:13:59.541
<v DOTSON>And we had a grocery store in Mossville . . . Let's see,

129
00:13:59.541 --> 00:14:06.166
I believe we had the first grocery store, and it was right in our front yard.

130
00:14:06.166 --> 00:14:07.408
<v COOPER>On Prater Road?

131
00:14:07.408 --> 00:14:09.478
<v DOTSON>On 378 [Old Spanish Road].

132
00:14:09.478 --> 00:14:10.720
<v COOPER>378 Prater Road.

133
00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:11.962
<v DOTSON>Yes [meant “no”].

134
00:14:11.962 --> 00:14:13.618
<v COOPER>And who owned it?

135
00:14:13.618 --> 00:14:14.446
<v DOTSON>My parents.

136
00:14:14.446 --> 00:14:15.274
<v COOPER>Your parents.

137
00:14:15.274 --> 00:14:15.688
<v DOTSON>[Agrees].

138
00:14:15.688 --> 00:14:19.000
<v COOPER>Do you remember what it was called?

139
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.461
<v DOTSON>No, I don't.

140
00:14:20.461 --> 00:14:23.868
I don't remember what it was called.

141
00:14:23.868 --> 00:14:26.303
I never thought about that.

142
00:14:26.303 --> 00:14:30.197
I guess just "the store" . . . I don't know.

143
00:14:30.197 --> 00:14:33.118
But, that never crossed my mind.

144
00:14:33.118 --> 00:14:41.882
<v COOPER>And what year, do you know around what time that opened and how long it was open for?

145
00:14:41.882 --> 00:14:51.618
<v DOTSON>No, you see I was very young then, so I don't know when it . . . The date that it actually opened.

146
00:14:51.618 --> 00:15:03.303
But I know we had a store and it was right in the front yard, and I was young then, when we did that.

147
00:15:03.303 --> 00:15:12.066
And now, in Mossville, now Mossville was a great place to grow up, I mean a great place.

148
00:15:12.066 --> 00:15:16.934
You didn’t have to worry about your children, or anything.

149
00:15:16.934 --> 00:15:23.750
And we didn't know what we didn't have, because we thought we had everything.

150
00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:26.671
We got a TV when . . . Oh

151
00:15:26.671 --> 00:15:36.408
<c.p>my God I don’t know what year that was, but we had a TV and it was a round screen.

152
00:15:36.408 --> 00:15:42.250
It was old-type, but it was a new TV for the time.

153
00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:53.934
And, I think we could only get a few stations and the TV would go off, I think like at ten o'clock at night.

154
00:15:53.934 --> 00:15:57.829
The station would just shut down, no . . . Nothing.

155
00:15:57.829 --> 00:16:00.263
The station's closed and everything.

156
00:16:00.263 --> 00:16:06.592
But I don't think we could get but maybe one or two stations.

157
00:16:06.592 --> 00:16:13.408
I know channel seven was one, that was KPLC, I remember that very good.

158
00:16:13.408 --> 00:16:19.737
And I remember watching Roy Rogers, and all of this kind of stuff.

159
00:16:19.737 --> 00:16:28.987
But my daddy was a very good provider so we had . . . I guess you'd consider us being middle class,

160
00:16:28.987 --> 00:16:40.184
but we didn't . . . You didn't carry what you had as being putting you better than anybody, because everybody was on the same level.

161
00:16:40.184 --> 00:16:47.000
If you had a problem in Mossville, you would go to my daddy.

162
00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:53.637
He would go to Henry Reed, they would get the problem solved.

163
00:16:53.637 --> 00:17:06.358
And if . . . People would like wake us up at all time in mornings and night to go get somebody out of jail [16:53-16:55:

164
00:17:06.358 --> 00:17:08.017
personal information omitted].

165
00:17:08.017 --> 00:17:12.442
But it was . . . Life was really, really good.

166
00:17:12.442 --> 00:17:16.314
My daddy died when I was seven,

167
00:17:16.314 --> 00:17:37.884
so I can't go too far back as to what happened before that because I was young and I didn’t really have no interest in knowing these things because I had my daddy and my mom and my sister.

168
00:17:37.884 --> 00:17:50.052
And we had a yard that . . . The yard, we didn't have any dividers it was just yards just ran into each other.

169
00:17:50.052 --> 00:17:55.030
And I had no young siblings, but there was,

170
00:17:55.030 --> 00:18:07.198
the Praters lived . . . My auntie lived next door and the Praters lived next door to them, and they had about eight kids.

171
00:18:07.198 --> 00:18:30.980
So I had sisters and brothers of my age that we associated with and it was really . . . Times was just great when we were growing up because we had no fear of people coming in your neighborhood and taking your children or anything.

172
00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:45.360
Because if you got in trouble or if somebody saw that you were farther than you should have been, playing, they went and told your parents.

173
00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.679
And you got brought back home.

174
00:18:48.679 --> 00:18:53.657
And my mother, my mother worked all the time.

175
00:18:53.657 --> 00:18:54.210
She

176
00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:59.188
<c.p>worked in Maplewood and they would walk to work.

177
00:18:59.188 --> 00:19:03.059
<v COOPER>And what did she do in Maplewood?

178
00:19:03.059 --> 00:19:04.719
<v DOTSON>They cleaned houses.

179
00:19:04.719 --> 00:19:18.546
And it was quite a few ladies from the Mossville area that worked over there, and then my daddy finally got my momma a car.

180
00:19:18.546 --> 00:19:26.289
And they . . . Well my mom didn't know how to drive so she taught herself.

181
00:19:26.289 --> 00:19:30.160
And my . . . CB, which is my brother,

182
00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:40.116
[his] wife was the only one that would ride with her because she didn't know how to drive.

183
00:19:40.116 --> 00:19:49.519
And that was her companion as far as a riding companion that would go anywhere with her.

184
00:19:49.519 --> 00:19:53.943
But she didn't go any farther than Maplewood.

185
00:19:53.943 --> 00:19:59.474
And interstate . . . they were building Interstate 10 when . . . Let's see,

186
00:19:59.474 --> 00:20:06.111
I was about maybe twelve when they were building on Interstate 10.

187
00:20:06.111 --> 00:20:17.173
I can remember that very well, because it was Highway 90 that was the main road then, before Interstate 10.

188
00:20:17.173 --> 00:20:24.363
And, we would have to cross Highway 90 to get to that area,

189
00:20:24.363 --> 00:20:31.000
which was . . . It was Prater Road would have run on across.

190
00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:42.380
And we used to ride down there on our bicycles and watching them build the interstate, which was . . . That was fun for us.

191
00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:47.822
I mean, to do that that was just something to see.

192
00:20:47.822 --> 00:20:56.233
And we had a . . . the Praters had a . . . They had a job, the kids had a job.

193
00:20:56.233 --> 00:21:04.149
They always . . . The kids always had an old car and the old car, they delivered newspapers.

194
00:21:04.149 --> 00:21:13.550
And they had a convertible, a convertible Ford and we would go to the . . . It was called The Midway,

195
00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:21.466
a drive in movie, and it was a dollar for four to get in the movie.

196
00:21:21.466 --> 00:21:30.866
And it was like, they had . . . you would . . . They had a white section and a colored section, they said.

197
00:21:30.866 --> 00:21:35.814
Okay, Jim Lee was the manager over the colored section.

198
00:21:35.814 --> 00:21:46.699
And the . . . they had speakers that was in the ground and you would have to . . . And it was like on a mound,

199
00:21:46.699 --> 00:21:52.636
and you’d drive your car up on the mound, like the front

200
00:21:52.636 --> 00:22:03.026
<c.p>wheels, and you would take one of these speakers off this thing in the middle and hang it on your window,

201
00:22:03.026 --> 00:22:05.995
so you could hear the movie.

202
00:22:05.995 --> 00:22:14.406
And it was always more than four of us in the car because we was slipping in,

203
00:22:14.406 --> 00:22:19.353
because we didn't have enough money for everybody to pay.

204
00:22:19.353 --> 00:22:37.660
And Mr. Jim Lee would come by and find all the [there was more than four] . . . We’d get put out of the drive-in because we had done slipped kids in there that wasn't supposed to be there.

205
00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:41.618
But that was alright, we didn't mind that.

206
00:22:41.618 --> 00:22:52.008
And I don’t think they told our parents because we didn't get in trouble for it, which we really enjoyed that.

207
00:22:52.008 --> 00:23:03.387
And when we were also growing up . . . Where my daddy had the pond built, where the dirt that they put on Prater Road?

208
00:23:03.387 --> 00:23:10.809
Well the Praters, June had a goat and June was going to build a boat.

209
00:23:10.809 --> 00:23:13.283
Well, June built a boat,

210
00:23:13.283 --> 00:23:26.147
and tied the . . . He took tin and made the shape of a boat and he took and [tarred?] some tin to the bottom of the boat.

211
00:23:26.147 --> 00:23:35.547
And the goat would pull the boat around the pond and too many of us got in the boat,

212
00:23:35.547 --> 00:23:40.000
and the bottom of the boat fell out.

213
00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:40.930
<v COOPER>Oh no!

214
00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:50.227
<v DOTSON>And we were all in the water [both laugh] but it wasn't that deep that . . . None of us got hurt,

215
00:23:50.227 --> 00:23:57.665
it was a miracle we didn't get hurt, because what we wouldn't try wasn't thought of.

216
00:23:57.665 --> 00:24:01.849
But it was really fun, we made our fun.

217
00:24:01.849 --> 00:24:12.076
Because we went to church, it was like . . . The religion was like the Sanctified Religion, the Baptists, the Methodists, and the Catholics.

218
00:24:12.076 --> 00:24:19.049
The Catholic Church was in Westlake [Louisiana], and the other churches was all in Mossville.

219
00:24:19.049 --> 00:24:27.416
And during that time, being young . . . Well the Catholic kids only went to church for one hour but,

220
00:24:27.416 --> 00:24:33.459
in the Sanctified Religion, it looked like we stayed in church all day.

221
00:24:33.459 --> 00:24:44.151
And the Methodists didn't stay in church too long, and the Baptists always had something going on for their kids, and the kids

222
00:24:44.151 --> 00:24:47.870
<c.p>from the Sanctified Church . . . Well, it was like,

223
00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:56.703
"I want to be Baptist" or "I want to be Catholic" because it seemed like their religion was fun.

224
00:24:56.703 --> 00:25:06.000
And we stayed all day, but we finally got used to what was going on after we got older.

225
00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:17.345
And we also used to have a bus that would come . . . I don't know who had the bus service in Mossville during that time,

226
00:25:17.345 --> 00:25:28.691
but it was a bus that would come from Westlake, and it would stop in Mossville and it would take us to Lake Charles.

227
00:25:28.691 --> 00:25:32.945
And I remember we used to catch the bus,

228
00:25:32.945 --> 00:25:44.764
they used to drop us off on the corner by Newberry's and that's where you had to catch the bus to come back to Mossville.

229
00:25:44.764 --> 00:25:50.436
And I think it cost like twenty-five cents to ride the bus.

230
00:25:50.436 --> 00:25:58.473
And on Saturdays, that was such a pleasure to be able to go to the . . . to Mossville,

231
00:25:58.473 --> 00:26:03.200
to the . . . To go to Lake Charles on the bus.

232
00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:12.182
And let's see what else we did . . . Oh, they had a fair that would come to Mossville every year.

233
00:26:12.182 --> 00:26:15.018
Every year there was a fair.

234
00:26:15.018 --> 00:26:23.527
And it was . . . We owned the property across the street, and the fair would come there every year.

235
00:26:23.527 --> 00:26:25.891
Just like the Cal-Cam Fair?

236
00:26:25.891 --> 00:26:28.727
This fair would come to Mossville.

237
00:26:28.727 --> 00:26:32.509
And that was . . . We looked forward to it.

238
00:26:32.509 --> 00:26:36.764
<v COOPER>And that was right across the street on Prater?

239
00:26:36.764 --> 00:26:46.691
<v DOTSON>Right on . . . It was on the corner of Prater and 378 [Old Spanish Road], which is across the street from us.

240
00:26:46.691 --> 00:26:48.582
We own that corner.

241
00:26:48.582 --> 00:26:50.000
<v COOPER>I see.

242
00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:52.439
<v DOTSON>So that's where the fair would set up every year.

243
00:26:52.439 --> 00:26:58.049
<v COOPER>Wow, so that was . . . Your home was a bustling place then, with the grocery store, and all of the kids, and the pond.

244
00:26:58.049 --> 00:26:58.293
<v DOTSON>Yes.

245
00:26:58.293 --> 00:27:03.415
<v COOPER>So next door when you talk about . . . When you're telling these stories of playing with the kids that live next door,

246
00:27:03.415 --> 00:27:05.854
those are the Rigmaidens, I mean, those are the Praters?

247
00:27:05.854 --> 00:27:06.098
<v DOTSON>Praters.

248
00:27:06.098 --> 00:27:10.000
<v COOPER>And did they help . . . Did any of you kids help with the grocery store or . . .?

249
00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:11.411
<v DOTSON>Yeah, my sister.

250
00:27:11.411 --> 00:27:12.351
<v COOPER>Your sister.

251
00:27:12.351 --> 00:27:17.053
<v DOTSON>My sister worked in the grocery store, and my mom.

252
00:27:17.053 --> 00:27:20.344
I didn't because I was too young.

253
00:27:20.344 --> 00:27:22.225
<v COOPER>What did they do?

254
00:27:22.225 --> 00:27:26.927
<v DOTSON>We had like a meat counter, I can remember that.

255
00:27:26.927 --> 00:27:36.801
And they had a heater in there, and they sold . . . I know they had a thing on the counter with cookies.

256
00:27:36.801 --> 00:27:38.682
Two . . . I remember two

257
00:27:38.682 --> 00:27:40.563
<c.p>cookies for a penny.

258
00:27:40.563 --> 00:27:45.265
And they was those coconut . . . They still have them now.

259
00:27:45.265 --> 00:27:47.146
These coconut . . . COOPER: Macaroons?

260
00:27:47.146 --> 00:27:47.616
<v DOTSON>Yeah.

261
00:27:47.616 --> 00:27:51.848
They had those cookies and people would buy cookies.

262
00:27:51.848 --> 00:28:00.781
Like the kids would come in and buy a slice of bologna, a slice of cheese, and the cookies.

263
00:28:00.781 --> 00:28:05.954
And they would buy sodas, I can remember them buying sodas.

264
00:28:05.954 --> 00:28:12.066
Well it was like bread, and it was just like a neighborhood store.

265
00:28:12.066 --> 00:28:15.828
And it was a gas station on it.

266
00:28:15.828 --> 00:28:16.298
<v COOPER>Wow.

267
00:28:16.298 --> 00:28:21.000
<v DOTSON>And they used to fix flats on the side . . .

268
00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:26.323
<v COOPER>Where did they get all of the meat and the produce from?

269
00:28:26.323 --> 00:28:32.532
<v DOTSON>It used to come from Lake Charles, from . . . I think it was Kelly Webbers?

270
00:28:32.532 --> 00:28:33.419
<v COOPER>Kelly Webbers?

271
00:28:33.419 --> 00:28:33.863
<v DOTSON>Yes.

272
00:28:33.863 --> 00:28:41.403
That place is called the . . . now they've turned it into a . . . It's like an entertainment center now.

273
00:28:41.403 --> 00:28:51.161
I can't think of what they called it now, but that's where produce . . . I mean where the stuff comes from, Kelly Webbers.

274
00:28:51.161 --> 00:28:58.702
I don't remember if my parents went and picked it up or if a truck delivered it,

275
00:28:58.702 --> 00:29:03.581
but I know a truck delivered the gas and the kerosene,

276
00:29:03.581 --> 00:29:06.685
<c.p>because kerosene was big at that time.

277
00:29:06.685 --> 00:29:08.903
And let's see what else . . .

278
00:29:08.903 --> 00:29:16.000
<v COOPER>Do you remember what brand of kerosene and gas or what the truck was called?

279
00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:18.481
<v DOTSON>No, I don't remember that.

280
00:29:18.481 --> 00:29:27.907
And it was like . . . Oh, and the Praters, Mr. Audrey Prater worked at Conoco [refinery in Lake Charles, Louisiana].

281
00:29:27.907 --> 00:29:38.326
And we didn't have a swimming pool, but he used to take us to Conoco Park, which is across the street.

282
00:29:38.326 --> 00:29:49.736
I don't know if it's . . . If that it is still there now or not but it was a square . . . cement pond like thing.

283
00:29:49.736 --> 00:29:58.171
And it had a water spout in the middle, and Mr. Audrey would bring us there swimming.

284
00:29:58.171 --> 00:30:07.597
It wasn’t really deep enough to swim, it was just deep enough for you to get in the water.

285
00:30:07.597 --> 00:30:14.543
And every year, he would take us to Beaumont [Texas], to the state fair.

286
00:30:14.543 --> 00:30:20.000
And that was . . . Oh my God that was a treat.

287
00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:28.049
So that's why I'm saying we didn't know what we didn't have, because we had so much.

288
00:30:28.049 --> 00:30:35.152
And we did so much and it was like . . . It was just like a family.

289
00:30:35.152 --> 00:30:37.993
I mean everybody was a family.

290
00:30:37.993 --> 00:30:49.830
And across the street is where . . . Across the street from the Prater’s house is where the Vincent's lived and they had four or five kids,

291
00:30:49.830 --> 00:30:55.512
but it was three of them that was in our age group.

292
00:30:55.512 --> 00:30:59.774
And we liked to sit down and play cards.

293
00:30:59.774 --> 00:31:05.456
We used to play Old Maids and we would play on Sunday.

294
00:31:05.456 --> 00:31:09.244
Everybody had to go to church on Sunday.

295
00:31:09.244 --> 00:31:18.240
It was no if you went to church; you went to church and you better act right in church.

296
00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:30.078
And we would play cards and the Vincent's mom wouldn't let them play cards with us on Sunday because she said it was a sin.

297
00:31:30.078 --> 00:31:32.919
But we wasn't gambling, you know?

298
00:31:32.919 --> 00:31:39.074
We were playing cards and we never could understand what her reasoning was.

299
00:31:39.074 --> 00:31:49.018
And we would always want to play on their front porch because their front porch was up high off the ground.

300
00:31:49.018 --> 00:31:59.908
<c.p>And we always liked to go over there and Mr. Beaux Collins, which was their daddy, worked at the . . . Worked for the railroad.

301
00:31:59.908 --> 00:32:03.223
And he would . . . We would pick muscadines.

302
00:32:03.223 --> 00:32:05.590
And he would make wine.

303
00:32:05.590 --> 00:32:13.166
But he wouldn't give us any, but we had to pick the wine . . . Pick the berries.

304
00:32:13.166 --> 00:32:15.534
And we enjoyed doing that.

305
00:32:15.534 --> 00:32:21.689
We also had a sugarcane mill in the neighborhood where they made syrup.

306
00:32:21.689 --> 00:32:26.424
And that was at the . . . Their name was Vincent too.

307
00:32:26.424 --> 00:32:29.265
That family was a Vincent too.

308
00:32:29.265 --> 00:32:34.000
And the sugarcane was grown on the Hartman's property.

309
00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:38.744
<v COOPER>And did . . . Did they ever sell any of the sugar cane syrup?

310
00:32:38.744 --> 00:32:39.140
<v DOTSON>Syrup?

311
00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:41.116
<v COOPER>Or the wine from the . . .?

312
00:32:41.116 --> 00:32:47.837
<v DOTSON>No, Mr. Beaux Collins didn't sell the wine he just . . . He drank it and gave it away.

313
00:32:47.837 --> 00:32:51.000
And I know they sold the syrup.

314
00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:52.286
<v COOPER>In your store?

315
00:32:52.286 --> 00:32:54.000
In your family store?

316
00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:58.714
<v DOTSON>No, it was just they bought it from the sugarcane mill.

317
00:32:58.714 --> 00:33:01.286
It wasn't . . . Nothing licensed you know.

318
00:33:01.286 --> 00:33:04.286
The sugarcane place wasn’t licensed or anything.

319
00:33:04.286 --> 00:33:22.714
They had a mule that would pull . . . They would stick the sugarcane in a hole in this thing and it would turn around with the donkey . . . mule pulling it and it would squeeze all of the syrup . . . the juice out of the sugarcane.

320
00:33:22.714 --> 00:33:27.000
And then they would cook it in these big vats.

321
00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.571
But I don't remember them doing . . .

322
00:33:29.571 --> 00:33:38.143
<c.p>They had to have sold it to people around in the neighborhood because I know it wasn't in the store.

323
00:33:38.143 --> 00:33:41.143
Well if it was I don't remember.

324
00:33:41.143 --> 00:33:46.286
But that was most of the stuff that went on in Mossville.

325
00:33:46.286 --> 00:33:51.429
Oh and my mom was a school bus driver after a while,

326
00:33:51.429 --> 00:34:00.000
and she would pick up the kids from Houston River [Area in Sulphur, Louisiana?] and bring them to Mossville.

327
00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:07.810
<v COOPER>Was she a school bus driver at the same time she was cleaning houses in Maplewood?

328
00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:11.227
<v DOTSON>No she had stopped cleaning houses then.

329
00:34:11.227 --> 00:34:33.194
And there was another bus that would come in from . . . After the school was established there was another bus that would come in from Sulphur that would bring the kids from the . . . I think the eighth grade to Mossville and the high school to Mossville.

330
00:34:33.194 --> 00:34:41.493
And my mom would pick up the kids out of Houston River and bring them to Mossville.

331
00:34:41.493 --> 00:34:48.815
And the kids from Westlake, I don't know how . . . What bus they came in on.

332
00:34:48.815 --> 00:34:55.161
But everything went in to Mossville, to the school, which was a powerhouse.

333
00:34:55.161 --> 00:35:04.924
The Mossville Pirates were really, really a good school because we had so many kids feeding in from different areas.

334
00:35:04.924 --> 00:35:12.735
And it was just . . . I don't know how to say it, but it was just great.

335
00:35:12.735 --> 00:35:21.521
Then they finally built us a stadium, because we used to have to use Westlake High School’s stadium.

336
00:35:21.521 --> 00:35:24.938
We didn't have one of our own.

337
00:35:24.938 --> 00:35:27.867
And my . . . And at the time,

338
00:35:27.867 --> 00:35:38.607
well that's when they built the gym then they built the stadium and they added on to the school, which still stands.

339
00:35:38.607 --> 00:35:43.000
That's where . . . Have you been to Mossville school?

340
00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:43.600
<v COOPER>[Agrees].

341
00:35:43.600 --> 00:35:50.200
I think . . . is that . . . that's right . . . Oh, we drove past it.

342
00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:56.800
We drove past it on the way to the Rigmaiden Center.

343
00:35:56.800 --> 00:35:58.600
<v DOTSON>That's the school.

344
00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:05.200
And it was one of the nicest schools in the area.

345
00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:10.600
White or black, it was really a fine school.

346
00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:14.800
And we were very proud of it.

347
00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:18.400
And the kids did real well.

348
00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:31.600
Because Mossville School was . . . the kids that . . . The teachers that came into Mossville School was like came from Grambling [University in Grambling,

349
00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:37.600
Louisiana] and they came from Southern [University and A&amp;M College].

350
00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:44.800
And like the teachers, our teachers wasn't much older than we were,

351
00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:53.200
because they got out of school like at twenty . . . College like twenty-two and twenty-three.

352
00:36:53.200 --> 00:37:00.400
And the kids, the average kid graduated from Mossville was like eighteen,

353
00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:08.800
and so it wasn't too much different in the teacher and the . . . [phone rings]

354
00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:09.400
<v COOPER>Uh-oh.

355
00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:13.000
<v DOTSON>Hey [?] can I call you back?

356
00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:14.200
It’s here.

357
00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:16.000
Alright, bye.

358
00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:16.494
<v COOPER>Alright.

359
00:37:16.494 --> 00:37:23.407
Yeah, if you could put it a little higher up like, maybe over here?

360
00:37:23.407 --> 00:37:24.395
Yeah perfect.

361
00:37:24.395 --> 00:37:28.346
We can make sure we get your voice.

362
00:37:28.346 --> 00:37:29.827
Oh my goodness.

363
00:37:29.827 --> 00:37:32.790
Okay so there . . . This is wonderful.

364
00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:38.716
So I'm curious to know a little bit more about your father.

365
00:37:38.716 --> 00:37:46.617
So he was a minister, but he also had the grocery store and the gas station.

366
00:37:46.617 --> 00:37:49.086
What else did he do?

367
00:37:49.086 --> 00:37:52.543
Is there anything else that he was . . .?

368
00:37:52.543 --> 00:37:58.469
<v DOTSON>Well like the mayor . . . People considered him being the mayor of Mossville.

369
00:37:58.469 --> 00:38:02.420
And he was a sheriff for Henry Reed.

370
00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:07.852
During that time he had to be one of the first,

371
00:38:07.852 --> 00:38:20.691
or if the first black sheriff that was a . . . and his whatever you want to call it . . . I don't know if he got paid or not.

372
00:38:20.691 --> 00:38:25.630
But I know he and Henry Reed were so close.

373
00:38:25.630 --> 00:38:29.580
And anything that he needed done he could

374
00:38:29.580 --> 00:38:40.938
<c.p>get it because he had a word, and he believed in a handshake and you didn't have to have anything written on paper.

375
00:38:40.938 --> 00:38:46.864
He gave his word that's . . . That was good as gold back then.

376
00:38:46.864 --> 00:38:56.247
And there were a lot of more people in Maplewood that . . . I mean they just really respected my daddy.

377
00:38:56.247 --> 00:39:01.185
He was a leader and he was a good guy.

378
00:39:01.185 --> 00:39:11.062
And that's all I really can . . . because I mean the name just . . . His name just spoke for who he was.

379
00:39:11.062 --> 00:39:16.988
And I'm very proud to be his daughter because he was really,

380
00:39:16.988 --> 00:39:25.383
really a good person and he . . . What he stood for, that made you respect him even more.

381
00:39:25.383 --> 00:39:35.259
And from where . . . During that time, being a black man and having the power that he did to help people,

382
00:39:35.259 --> 00:39:46.123
and to understand what people were coming from, and to be able to help them it's really . . . It was really a joy.

383
00:39:46.123 --> 00:39:56.000
Because he was really there for everybody, and he was just respected by everybody, and it was really great.

384
00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:00.080
<v COOPER>And you showed me the card earlier, his official deputy sheriff card.

385
00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:04.160
So he had that for he was officially . . . What was the title?

386
00:40:04.160 --> 00:40:06.540
It was the deputy of Calcasieu Parish?

387
00:40:06.540 --> 00:40:07.220
<v DOTSON>Parish, yeah.

388
00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:09.940
<v COOPER>And he didn't get paid to do that?

389
00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:11.640
<v DOTSON>I don't think he did.

390
00:40:11.640 --> 00:40:13.000
I don't know.

391
00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:16.037
<v COOPER>Do you remember him telling any stories?

392
00:40:16.037 --> 00:40:19.942
Or do you remember any . . . any of the experiences?

393
00:40:19.942 --> 00:40:27.751
<v DOTSON>All I remember is that if you got in trouble in Mossville . . . Well they called him Cousin Josh.

394
00:40:27.751 --> 00:40:31.656
Go to Cousin Josh and he'll help you out.

395
00:40:31.656 --> 00:40:38.598
Because I know one of the Braxtons . . . Daniel Braxton used to get in trouble a lot.

396
00:40:38.598 --> 00:40:40.333
I mean a lot.

397
00:40:40.333 --> 00:40:45.106
And when my daddy died, he told my mom he said,

398
00:40:45.106 --> 00:40:55.952
"I'm going to have to stop getting in trouble because Cousin Josh is gone, and I won't have anybody to get me out of jail."

399
00:40:55.952 --> 00:41:07.233
And I don't think he got into any more trouble, because he was like a bad kid and he knew my daddy would get him out.

400
00:41:07.233 --> 00:41:15.042
And but he was . . . he came from . . . They were kin to us; the Braxtons are kin to us.

401
00:41:15.042 --> 00:41:18.079
Well really everybody almost in that neighborhood.

402
00:41:18.079 --> 00:41:21.116
Intertwined with relatives marrying relatives and everything.

403
00:41:21.116 --> 00:41:26.757
So it really was . . . It was just Mossville was like a big family.

404
00:41:26.757 --> 00:41:29.794
And my daddy . . . my grandfather . . . Let's see.

405
00:41:29.794 --> 00:41:35.000
My daddy's daddy, that would have been my grandfather, was white.

406
00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:37.691
<v COOPER>And what was his name?

407
00:41:37.691 --> 00:41:46.302
<v DOTSON>I don't know if it was Henry Rigmaiden but Karl . . . Carol and them would know that.

408
00:41:46.302 --> 00:41:50.608
But they came out of the West [Fork?].

409
00:41:50.608 --> 00:42:02.448
But like the Ryans, the [Salgays?], the [Bells?], these are all white families but they met . . . See they married during that time.

410
00:42:02.448 --> 00:42:08.368
So they married and that became . . . All of us became kin.

411
00:42:08.368 --> 00:42:19.132
And now, there's a lot of white Rigmaidens don't want to be kin to us, but every Rigmaiden is kin.

412
00:42:19.132 --> 00:42:23.438
White, black, or whatever; we are all kin.

413
00:42:23.438 --> 00:42:27.743
But a lot of them don't want to,

414
00:42:27.743 --> 00:42:41.736
but maybe now they might reconsider because like in DeQuincy [Louisiana] there's a lot of Rigmaidens and they just flat don't want to accept that fact.

415
00:42:41.736 --> 00:42:43.889
But it's a fact.

416
00:42:43.889 --> 00:42:47.118
But we don't push the issue.

417
00:42:47.118 --> 00:42:59.497
I mean if you want to be kin to us or if you don’t want to say you kin to us that's fine.

418
00:42:59.497 --> 00:43:04.878
If you don't want to we still know the truth.

419
00:43:04.878 --> 00:43:07.031
So it didn't matter.

420
00:43:07.031 --> 00:43:11.875
And it was . . . That's just how life was then.

421
00:43:11.875 --> 00:43:19.948
<c.p>Oh yeah we had a . . . I forgot, well it was a night club in Mossville.

422
00:43:19.948 --> 00:43:26.944
Well they had more than one, but they had one called the Paradise.

423
00:43:26.944 --> 00:43:29.097
Valery Montgomery owned that.

424
00:43:29.097 --> 00:43:35.017
And every big name artist during that time came to Mossville.

425
00:43:35.017 --> 00:43:36.094
Like B.B.

426
00:43:36.094 --> 00:43:40.938
King, Ray Charles . . . let's see who else . . . Etta James.

427
00:43:40.938 --> 00:43:46.319
Let’s see who else . . . I mean just all of them.

428
00:43:46.319 --> 00:43:49.549
They all came to The Paradise.

429
00:43:49.549 --> 00:43:52.778
So Mossville was just up there.

430
00:43:52.778 --> 00:43:56.007
You name it we had it.

431
00:43:56.007 --> 00:44:04.080
We had a great school, we had a great community, we had good public relations,

432
00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:10.000
we had a good . . . We was getting a good education.

433
00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:15.813
And the education was . . . It wasn't second class, but we got hand-me-down books.

434
00:44:15.813 --> 00:44:18.048
But we learned . . . You learned.

435
00:44:18.048 --> 00:44:23.861
I mean you learned, because the teachers were so instrumental on us learning.

436
00:44:23.861 --> 00:44:30.568
Because when school . . . When it first started out in Mossville we only had three teachers.

437
00:44:30.568 --> 00:44:36.381
One was Ms. Washington, one was Ms. [Billips?], and one was Ms. Mitchell.

438
00:44:36.381 --> 00:44:39.958
And you learned what you had to learn.

439
00:44:39.958 --> 00:44:49.795
I don't care if you didn't have good books, you wasn't going to be a dummy because that was important to them,

440
00:44:49.795 --> 00:44:59.631
and all they had to was tell your parents you weren't acting right, or you didn't act like you wanted to learn.

441
00:44:59.631 --> 00:45:04.550
When next day you went to school, you were no problem.

442
00:45:04.550 --> 00:45:12.151
Because I think education back then was so important, because they didn't have a lot of education.

443
00:45:12.151 --> 00:45:19.752
Because my mom . . . I think my dad went to third grade, and he could write his name.

444
00:45:19.752 --> 00:45:27.353
He could do . . . He could read, he could write, he could drive, he could do it all.

445
00:45:27.353 --> 00:45:32.272
And my mom, I don't know what grade she stopped in,

446
00:45:32.272 --> 00:45:39.873
but they were so rural and in Texas that they worked in the fields all the time.

447
00:45:39.873 --> 00:45:51.051
So they didn't have too much education because it was ten girls and no boys and so they were . . . They worked hard in the field.

448
00:45:51.051 --> 00:46:07.148
And mama laughed . . . Mama would laugh and say she was so happy to get out of Texas that when my daddy asked her to marry him she said she didn't even have to think about it

449
00:46:07.148 --> 00:46:11.619
<c.p>because she knew she was getting out of the fields.

450
00:46:11.619 --> 00:46:16.538
And she laughed . . . She would laugh about that all the time.

451
00:46:16.538 --> 00:46:21.456
And she said she didn't care how old my daddy was.

452
00:46:21.456 --> 00:46:28.163
She learned to love him because he was a good person and a good provider.

453
00:46:28.163 --> 00:46:38.000
And he really took good care of my mom because she was really a young woman when she came to Mossville.

454
00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:42.577
<v COOPER>Do you remember what year they were married?

455
00:46:42.577 --> 00:46:44.293
<v DOTSON>No, I don't.

456
00:46:44.293 --> 00:46:46.581
My sister might know.

457
00:46:46.581 --> 00:46:54.019
And another thing about my mom, she loved to take care of people.

458
00:46:54.019 --> 00:46:58.595
She . . . Like the graveyard in Mossville, Morning Cemetery.

459
00:46:58.595 --> 00:47:03.172
My mom wanted to kind of like upgrade,

460
00:47:03.172 --> 00:47:15.758
and she would ask people that had people in the . . . Buried in the cemetery to come and help her paint the graves,

461
00:47:15.758 --> 00:47:20.335
repair the graves, and just keep it clean.

462
00:47:20.335 --> 00:47:32.349
Well she got a little help, but she said, "I'm not going to keep begging people to help me do this."

463
00:47:32.349 --> 00:47:43.791
So she bought all the paint and she would . . . She had this guy that lived not too far from her.

464
00:47:43.791 --> 00:47:51.228
Used to go with her to the graveyard, and they painted the graves.

465
00:47:51.228 --> 00:48:02.670
They . . . Every two weeks my mom took her own money and paid somebody, paid Archie [Prior?] to cut the graveyard.

466
00:48:02.670 --> 00:48:12.395
And she would go down there and she would sit and be sure it was done right.

467
00:48:12.395 --> 00:48:22.693
And she paid to have the graves cleaned and painted on her own, out of her own pocket.

468
00:48:22.693 --> 00:48:33.563
And after she died . . . Well it's grown up a lot and I would like to do more back there,

469
00:48:33.563 --> 00:48:37.567
but it's so secluded until it’s dangerous.

470
00:48:37.567 --> 00:48:41.000
<v COOPER>Where is the cemetery located?

471
00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:47.500
<v DOTSON>It's in Mossville, and it was behind the church which was torn down.

472
00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:56.000
But it's . . . I had talked to . . . I can't think of his name . . . At Sasol about the graveyard.

473
00:48:56.000 --> 00:48:57.500
And I asked

474
00:48:57.500 --> 00:49:09.500
<c.p>him was it possible . . . Could he find out who owned the property in the front of the graveyard so it could be cleaned out,

475
00:49:09.500 --> 00:49:16.000
so it wouldn't be . . . You could see all the way to the graveyard.

476
00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:22.500
And now the parish kept the road up to get to the graveyard.

477
00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:33.500
They put a . . . There's a really nice bridge they put there and people used to go back there and dump their trash.

478
00:49:33.500 --> 00:49:45.500
And my mom was able to get a gate put there so you couldn't get . . . you'd have . . . They would just throw their trash in.

479
00:49:45.500 --> 00:49:52.000
That was before the trash had to be picked up door to door.

480
00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:57.500
And . . . But after she died the graveyard has just gone down.

481
00:49:57.500 --> 00:50:05.000
It's because nobody is doing what my mom took it on her own to do.

482
00:50:05.000 --> 00:50:09.000
And she was . . . That was her pet peeve.

483
00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:12.000
She really enjoyed doing that.

484
00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:22.986
And she . . . Like a lot of the guys or the young men that rode her bus lived in the neighborhood that was on dope and didn't have money,

485
00:50:22.986 --> 00:50:24.556
she would feed them.

486
00:50:24.556 --> 00:50:31.226
And then one night she used to plant greens, vegetable garden along the front of her property.

487
00:50:31.226 --> 00:50:36.326
And one night somebody came along and stole . . . Pulled all her greens up.

488
00:50:36.326 --> 00:50:39.073
Stole . . . Pulled them up from the roots.

489
00:50:39.073 --> 00:50:43.389
And oh she was angry, she was very angry about that.

490
00:50:43.389 --> 00:50:48.097
And she called the sheriff department they came out they asked them.

491
00:50:48.097 --> 00:50:50.844
She said, "I know who did it.

492
00:50:50.844 --> 00:50:53.198
I found out who did it."

493
00:50:53.198 --> 00:50:57.906
Because see everybody . . . All of them loved her because she'd feed them.

494
00:50:57.906 --> 00:51:07.323
And she found out who did it and the cops said, "Well you want me to arrest them or what you want to do?"

495
00:51:07.323 --> 00:51:11.247
She said, "No. I don't want you to arrest them."

496
00:51:11.247 --> 00:51:14.385
Because she said, "What good would it do?

497
00:51:14.385 --> 00:51:23.017
They'd done either ate the greens or sold the greens and if you turn them upside down and shake them all day,

498
00:51:23.017 --> 00:51:26.156
a dime won't fall out of their pockets.

499
00:51:26.156 --> 00:51:30.080
So what are you going to get out of them?

500
00:51:30.080 --> 00:51:30.472
Nothing."

501
00:51:30.472 --> 00:51:32.434
So she let that go.

502
00:51:32.434 --> 00:51:36.358
But the other guys, that didn't set well with them.

503
00:51:36.358 --> 00:51:38.712
That person stealing my mom's greens.

504
00:51:38.712 --> 00:51:41.458
And but she soon got over it.

505
00:51:41.458 --> 00:51:47.344
And the guy who stole them she let him still come back and feed him.

506
00:51:47.344 --> 00:51:51.267
So this was the kind of parents that I had.

507
00:51:51.267 --> 00:51:53.229
And it was really good.

508
00:51:53.229 --> 00:51:55.191
It was really, really good.

509
00:51:55.191 --> 00:51:57.153
She loved all of us.

510
00:51:57.153 --> 00:51:58.722
I have two children.

511
00:51:58.722 --> 00:51:59.899
Which she loved.

512
00:51:59.899 --> 00:52:00.684
And my

513
00:52:00.684 --> 00:52:02.253
<c.p>sister had two children.

514
00:52:02.253 --> 00:52:05.000
<v COOPER>And what are your children's names?

515
00:52:05.000 --> 00:52:09.917
<v DOTSON>My daughter name is [Deidra?] and my son name is David.

516
00:52:09.917 --> 00:52:13.940
And my sister . . . Okay well Joshua is my sister's.

517
00:52:13.940 --> 00:52:15.281
He's the oldest.

518
00:52:15.281 --> 00:52:26.457
And what Deidra is the next oldest which is mine, Jackie is my sister’s, which she’s the third one, and David is the fourth one.

519
00:52:26.457 --> 00:52:27.798
Which is mine.

520
00:52:27.798 --> 00:52:33.162
So they were brought up like they were all sisters and brothers.

521
00:52:33.162 --> 00:52:45.678
But my mom was so good to them and she'd go to the store and she would have . . . she said she couldn't . . . Well her eyesight got real bad,

522
00:52:45.678 --> 00:52:47.913
she was a bad diabetic.

523
00:52:47.913 --> 00:52:52.830
Her got eyesight got really bad and she just stopped driving.

524
00:52:52.830 --> 00:52:56.407
She said she wasn't putting anybody in danger.

525
00:52:56.407 --> 00:52:58.195
She just stopped driving.

526
00:52:58.195 --> 00:53:08.923
She had a 1957 Impala and we used to call it the banana and she had that car looking like it was a truck.

527
00:53:08.923 --> 00:53:10.711
You could ask her.

528
00:53:10.711 --> 00:53:19.651
She had tools in the back, she had paint in there, she had a saw in there . . . A power saw.

529
00:53:19.651 --> 00:53:26.803
She had a chainsaw, she had all of this in the back of that old car.

530
00:53:26.803 --> 00:53:30.380
And she had Archie to drive her around.

531
00:53:30.380 --> 00:53:38.873
And if Burger King had a sale on hamburgers, man she'd go buy a whole big bag of them.

532
00:53:38.873 --> 00:53:49.154
And she would come here and give me so many, and she would go to my sister's give her so many of them.

533
00:53:49.154 --> 00:53:52.283
I mean this is how she was.

534
00:53:52.283 --> 00:53:56.753
Or if she would . . . She might would go buy chicken.

535
00:53:56.753 --> 00:54:01.671
She'd bring me a box, she'd bring my sister a box.

536
00:54:01.671 --> 00:54:07.929
Or if the man came around selling sweet potatoes my mama would buy them.

537
00:54:07.929 --> 00:54:18.657
Every time he'd come around she'd buy a sack of sweet potatoes knowing good and well she wasn't going to do anything with them.

538
00:54:18.657 --> 00:54:23.127
She would call me to come get the sweet potatoes.

539
00:54:23.127 --> 00:54:26.256
I would go get the sweet potatoes.

540
00:54:26.256 --> 00:54:29.832
I would take and peel them, boil them.

541
00:54:29.832 --> 00:54:40.114
And by the . . . By November I might have fifty potato pies I’d done made in my freezer because when mama kept buying sweet

542
00:54:40.114 --> 00:54:40.561
<c.p>potatoes.

543
00:54:40.561 --> 00:54:42.796
And so I told her.

544
00:54:42.796 --> 00:54:46.819
I said, "Mama what days does that man come?"

545
00:54:46.819 --> 00:54:48.607
And she told me.

546
00:54:48.607 --> 00:54:51.289
I said . . . I told my husband.

547
00:54:51.289 --> 00:55:00.229
I says, "I'm going to go over there and tell that man don't sell my mama any more sweet potatoes,

548
00:55:00.229 --> 00:55:06.040
because I'm tired of making pies and she's not doing anything with them."

549
00:55:06.040 --> 00:55:10.511
And so we finally got her stopped after a while.

550
00:55:10.511 --> 00:55:20.792
But if the man came by the next year she was going to buy more, but she didn't buy no more that year.

551
00:55:20.792 --> 00:55:25.262
And then she planted fruit trees all over her yard.

552
00:55:25.262 --> 00:55:28.838
Then we would have to go pick them.

553
00:55:28.838 --> 00:55:34.649
Well it was mainly me, because my sister wasn't going to do it.

554
00:55:34.649 --> 00:55:36.437
She would have pears.

555
00:55:36.437 --> 00:55:44.037
I'd have to go pick pears, I'd have to pick pecans, I'd have to go pick grapes.

556
00:55:44.037 --> 00:55:49.401
And I said, "Mama I'm tired of doing all of this stuff."

557
00:55:49.401 --> 00:55:52.083
I said, "You don't want it."

558
00:55:52.083 --> 00:55:57.000
She said, "No, I don't want it that's for y'all.

559
00:55:57.000 --> 00:55:59.909
<v COOPER>And this is after the store closed?

560
00:55:59.909 --> 00:56:00.325
So . . .

561
00:56:00.325 --> 00:56:01.156
<v DOTSON>Oh yes.

562
00:56:01.156 --> 00:56:08.221
This is when she had remarried and had moved from our house to a 4244 East Burton.

563
00:56:08.221 --> 00:56:09.052
<v COOPER>Oh okay.

564
00:56:09.052 --> 00:56:11.545
So this is on East Burton?

565
00:56:11.545 --> 00:56:14.039
<v DOTSON>Yeah she lived on East Burton.

566
00:56:14.039 --> 00:56:17.364
That was right down the street from us.

567
00:56:17.364 --> 00:56:21.519
See because Old Spanish Trail turn in to East Burton.

568
00:56:21.519 --> 00:56:27.753
If you were on the east side of Prater Road it was Old Spanish Trail,

569
00:56:27.753 --> 00:56:33.571
and if it was to the west side of it, it was East Burton.

570
00:56:33.571 --> 00:56:38.558
So that was the dividing line between Westlake and Sulphur; Prater Road.

571
00:56:38.558 --> 00:56:43.545
<v COOPER>Did she have trees and vegetable gardens on your old property too?

572
00:56:43.545 --> 00:56:43.961
<v DOTSON>Yeah.

573
00:56:43.961 --> 00:56:46.039
She had a big garden.

574
00:56:46.039 --> 00:56:49.779
She had chickens and all this kind of stuff.

575
00:56:49.779 --> 00:56:56.429
My mom was just . . . Like I said, my mom and my daddy was two special people.

576
00:56:56.429 --> 00:57:01.000
They were just two special people that enjoyed helping people.

577
00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:01.460
<v COOPER>Yeah.

578
00:57:01.460 --> 00:57:07.904
So your mom would she cook with all of the stuff that she'd grow?

579
00:57:07.904 --> 00:57:08.824
Was she . . .?

580
00:57:08.824 --> 00:57:14.808
<v DOTSON>She could cook, but she taught me to cook when I was young.

581
00:57:14.808 --> 00:57:15.268
<v COOPER>Okay.

582
00:57:15.268 --> 00:57:20.331
<v DOTSON>And she used to . . . She drove a school bus for years.

583
00:57:20.331 --> 00:57:22.632
She owned her own bus.

584
00:57:22.632 --> 00:57:30.916
And she would leave . . . I couldn't cook too much, but she would like . . . I would like, cook rice.

585
00:57:30.916 --> 00:57:38.741
I could cook rice, and I could fry meat and I was always trying to help her,

586
00:57:38.741 --> 00:57:43.803
because my daddy was dead and my mama hadn't remarried yet.

587
00:57:43.803 --> 00:57:53.469
So I was always trying to make it easy on my mom by helping her do things that I could do.

588
00:57:53.469 --> 00:57:55.310
I was like . . . tomboyish.

589
00:57:55.310 --> 00:57:58.071
I mean my sister was girly.

590
00:57:58.071 --> 00:57:59.452
But I wasn't.

591
00:57:59.452 --> 00:58:10.038
I mean I would do . . . Like right now I do a lot of a lot of different things that most ladies wouldn't do.

592
00:58:10.038 --> 00:58:15.561
After my husband died I just . . . This is how I came up.

593
00:58:15.561 --> 00:58:23.845
This is how I came up and I just carried it on over and it is a blessing,

594
00:58:23.845 --> 00:58:30.749
because you don't have to be as dependent on people to do everything for you.

595
00:58:30.749 --> 00:58:33.050
I'll try to do it.

596
00:58:33.050 --> 00:58:38.573
If I mess up well then I know I can’t do that.

597
00:58:38.573 --> 00:58:41.335
But I enjoy trying, because this

598
00:58:41.335 --> 00:58:44.096
<c.p>is how I was brought up.

599
00:58:44.096 --> 00:58:46.858
And it was really a pleasure.

600
00:58:46.858 --> 00:58:51.000
And I learned a lot from my parents.

601
00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:55.843
<v COOPER>What sorts of things do you remember learning from them?

602
00:58:55.843 --> 00:59:01.170
<v DOTSON>Well I learned how to cook, my mom taught me that,

603
00:59:01.170 --> 00:59:08.918
and to be independent that was the main thing, and how to get along with people.

604
00:59:08.918 --> 00:59:16.182
And I think I instilled this in my children, how to get along with people.

605
00:59:16.182 --> 00:59:23.931
And my . . . niece and nephew are the same way because they got a lot of value.

606
00:59:23.931 --> 00:59:31.195
I mean they are very respectful people, and my sister is a very respectable person.

607
00:59:31.195 --> 00:59:33.616
She owns a funeral home.

608
00:59:33.616 --> 00:59:38.943
It's James Funeral Home, which she inherited that from her husband.

609
00:59:38.943 --> 00:59:42.818
And she was the . . . My sister's a teacher.

610
00:59:42.818 --> 00:59:45.239
Was a teacher, she's retired.

611
00:59:45.239 --> 00:59:47.660
And she's very easy going.

612
00:59:47.660 --> 00:59:49.113
Very easy going.

613
00:59:49.113 --> 00:59:51.535
But we're two different people.

614
00:59:51.535 --> 01:00:01.220
I mean we both have a lot of values but she's . . . She don't get out there and cut her yard.

615
01:00:01.220 --> 01:00:04.610
She's never done this kind of stuff.

616
01:00:04.610 --> 01:00:08.000
She's always been on the girly type.

617
01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:18.654
I was on the tomboyish type, because it was just the two of us with my mom and we were both girls.

618
01:00:18.654 --> 01:00:26.403
So I had to do what I had to do to help her, so I thought.

619
01:00:26.403 --> 01:00:31.730
And like I said my sister was . . . She went to McNeese.

620
01:00:31.730 --> 01:00:36.572
She finished from over there and it was just great.

621
01:00:36.572 --> 01:00:38.509
It was just great.

622
01:00:38.509 --> 01:00:42.868
We had a good . . . We have a good family.

623
01:00:42.868 --> 01:00:43.836
We're close.

624
01:00:43.836 --> 01:00:45.289
We're closely knitted.

625
01:00:45.289 --> 01:00:50.616
We're not a big family, but we really enjoy each other.

626
01:00:50.616 --> 01:00:56.912
Because everybody turned out to be great people and I'm proud of that.

627
01:00:56.912 --> 01:01:04.176
I'm proud of my children, I'm proud of my nieces, I'm proud of my sister,

628
01:01:04.176 --> 01:01:11.925
and all of my nephews, my half nephews or whatever you want to address them as.

629
01:01:11.925 --> 01:01:14.830
But we're still one solid family.

630
01:01:14.830 --> 01:01:17.736
And I thank God for that.

631
01:01:17.736 --> 01:01:25.000
I thank God for who I am, and I know my children do too.

632
01:01:25.000 --> 01:01:32.293
And being brought up during that time and being black and being white we . . .

633
01:01:32.293 --> 01:01:36.981
<c.p>It was a lot of stuff we couldn't do.

634
01:01:36.981 --> 01:01:42.711
But it didn't hurt us as bad because we weren't . . . slaves.

635
01:01:42.711 --> 01:01:55.733
And it was just like one man told me one day he said, a white guy, he told me, he said, "Della, I can imagine."

636
01:01:55.733 --> 01:01:58.338
He said, "I can imagine.

637
01:01:58.338 --> 01:02:03.026
I can put myself in a black person's position."

638
01:02:03.026 --> 01:02:12.923
He said, "I can understand being black because I was . . ." He, the man, said he was brought up poor.

639
01:02:12.923 --> 01:02:23.862
I said, "You cannot understand being brought up black as no more than I can understand you being brought up poor."

640
01:02:23.862 --> 01:02:29.592
I said, "Because I don't know what being poor is like."

641
01:02:29.592 --> 01:02:36.363
I said, "I've never wanted nor needed anything that my parents didn't provide."

642
01:02:36.363 --> 01:02:40.531
I said, "I've never gone to bed hungry.

643
01:02:40.531 --> 01:02:44.698
I've always had a roof over my head.

644
01:02:44.698 --> 01:02:49.907
I had a mother and a father that loved me."

645
01:02:49.907 --> 01:02:53.032
I said, "I always had clothes.”

646
01:02:53.032 --> 01:03:00.846
I said, "We always . . . We didn't have a lot of money, but we wasn't broke."

647
01:03:00.846 --> 01:03:03.450
And it was . . . I said,

648
01:03:03.450 --> 01:03:20.640
"So you can’t understand where . . . I can't understand you being poor and not having shoes no more than you can say you know how I feel being black because the two don't match.

649
01:03:20.640 --> 01:03:25.849
Because I'd never been poor, and you've never been black.

650
01:03:25.849 --> 01:03:28.974
So you can't say you know.

651
01:03:28.974 --> 01:03:33.141
You don't know because I don't know either.

652
01:03:33.141 --> 01:03:35.746
So he looked at me.

653
01:03:35.746 --> 01:03:38.350
He says, "You know what?

654
01:03:38.350 --> 01:03:40.434
You got a point."

655
01:03:40.434 --> 01:03:41.997
I said, "Really?"

656
01:03:41.997 --> 01:03:51.373
I said, "But I can understand what loving you and you loving me as a person is like.

657
01:03:51.373 --> 01:03:58.145
But far as me being poor . . . Me being black and you being poor.

658
01:03:58.145 --> 01:04:00.228
That's not a comparison."

659
01:04:00.228 --> 01:04:07.000
Which it really wasn't, because I don't know what it was like.

660
01:04:07.000 --> 01:04:14.677
<v COOPER>Do you have some poignant moments or experiences growing up where you . . . Where you experienced being black?

661
01:04:14.677 --> 01:04:15.129
<v DOTSON>Well . . .

662
01:04:15.129 --> 01:04:16.032
<v COOPER>In Mossville?

663
01:04:16.032 --> 01:04:21.000
In a community or maybe in the larger Louisiana community?

664
01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:22.747
<v DOTSON>Not in Mossville.

665
01:04:22.747 --> 01:04:30.316
Now I know like, we used to go to the movies in Sulphur,

666
01:04:30.316 --> 01:04:41.379
I think it was called The Star, and we would have to sit upstairs, and they would sit downstairs.

667
01:04:41.379 --> 01:04:43.708
That was a separation.

668
01:04:43.708 --> 01:04:46.037
But you know what?

669
01:04:46.037 --> 01:04:49.530
We didn't hate anybody for it.

670
01:04:49.530 --> 01:04:53.023
We didn't know no other life.

671
01:04:53.023 --> 01:04:58.264
And like you couldn't go like to a restaurant,

672
01:04:58.264 --> 01:05:09.326
and like if you went to like a Dairy Queen they had signs in the stores, “White and colored.”

673
01:05:09.326 --> 01:05:12.820
There wasn’t . . . You wasn’t called black.

674
01:05:12.820 --> 01:05:14.567
You were colored.

675
01:05:14.567 --> 01:05:23.883
And you like at the movies the drive-in theater was like the white and the colored.

676
01:05:23.883 --> 01:05:52.995
But you know, with the signs and having to go to the back doors and do these kinds of things yeah it was there but we had so much in our neighborhood and communities and we could go . . . We went to the movies and we didn't . . . We didn't feel hurt.

677
01:05:52.995 --> 01:06:04.057
I mean I don't know if we didn't have enough sense or our parents didn't teach us racial stuff.

678
01:06:04.057 --> 01:06:09.298
See and I guess that made a big difference.

679
01:06:09.298 --> 01:06:16.867
We knew the white kids could do more than us outside our neighborhood.

680
01:06:16.867 --> 01:06:20.360
But you see what I'm saying?

681
01:06:20.360 --> 01:06:23.854
Mossville was where you had it.

682
01:06:23.854 --> 01:06:32.005
And being black . . . People had cars, you could go places, you could do things.

683
01:06:32.005 --> 01:06:37.828
But the signs didn't mean . . . We knew what it meant.

684
01:06:37.828 --> 01:06:51.219
We knew what we . . . We knew our limitations, but it wasn’t like in other areas where there was slavery and all of this.

685
01:06:51.219 --> 01:06:54.131
We don't know about that.

686
01:06:54.131 --> 01:06:57.042
I don't know about that.

687
01:06:57.042 --> 01:07:04.029
And in my neighborhood they didn't . . . The kids weren't treated like that.

688
01:07:04.029 --> 01:07:15.091
So I just don’t know what to say about that because like I said, we would go to Beaumont.

689
01:07:15.091 --> 01:07:17.420
Go to the fair.

690
01:07:17.420 --> 01:07:22.078
Mr. Audrey would bring us to the pool.

691
01:07:22.078 --> 01:07:27.901
We couldn't go to the white pool because we knew.

692
01:07:27.901 --> 01:07:29.648
We just knew.

693
01:07:29.648 --> 01:07:40.710
See, and being brought up in the fifties segregation was big and . . . But our parents didn't teach us that.

694
01:07:40.710 --> 01:07:47.697
They didn't teach us that the white kids were better than us.

695
01:07:47.697 --> 01:07:50.608
So that was a blessing.

696
01:07:50.608 --> 01:07:57.595
<c.p>That really was a blessing that they didn't teach that to us.

697
01:07:57.595 --> 01:08:04.000
And we just kept going and being happy and which . . .

698
01:08:04.000 --> 01:08:05.040
<v COOPER>Do you . . .?

699
01:08:05.040 --> 01:08:06.080
Oh sorry.

700
01:08:06.080 --> 01:08:07.120
<v DOTSON>Go ahead.

701
01:08:07.120 --> 01:08:19.080
And that was . . . That was a good thing being brought up in the Mossville community because my mom worked for the white people,

702
01:08:19.080 --> 01:08:22.720
everybody mostly worked for the white people.

703
01:08:22.720 --> 01:08:33.120
But if you wanted to open up a business like cutting wood and taking in washing and . . . This was fine.

704
01:08:33.120 --> 01:08:39.880
I mean but other areas . . . Crime was not a thing in our neighborhood.

705
01:08:39.880 --> 01:08:41.960
You didn't have crime.

706
01:08:41.960 --> 01:08:44.040
You didn't have drugs.

707
01:08:44.040 --> 01:08:45.600
They had wine.

708
01:08:45.600 --> 01:08:49.240
You know, liquor, but it wasn't drugs.

709
01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:56.520
And then Mossville really fell apart when a lot of new comers came in.

710
01:08:56.520 --> 01:08:58.600
That's when Mossville changed.

711
01:08:58.600 --> 01:09:10.040
But Mossville was one of the best places you could bring up a family because we weren't taught that we were poor.

712
01:09:10.040 --> 01:09:22.000
And we wasn't taught that the white kids were better than us because they just didn't . . . My parents just didn't do that.

713
01:09:22.000 --> 01:09:33.870
<v COOPER>Did they ever talk about their experiences working elsewhere, and talk about some of the discrimination they faced outside of Mossville?

714
01:09:33.870 --> 01:09:42.348
<v DOTSON>No. My mom didn't because my mom was born on the farm with ten girls.

715
01:09:42.348 --> 01:09:45.174
I mean in rural Texas.

716
01:09:45.174 --> 01:09:51.391
And my daddy never said anything because his parents were white.

717
01:09:51.391 --> 01:09:53.087
<v COOPER>Both of them?

718
01:09:53.087 --> 01:09:57.609
<v DOTSON>No Grandma [Linney?] the picture . . . She was black.

719
01:09:57.609 --> 01:10:06.652
But his daddy . . . see with the . . . They were the Rigmaidens that was on the West Fork.

720
01:10:06.652 --> 01:10:10.609
What they were doing . . . They had money.

721
01:10:10.609 --> 01:10:14.000
They had everything that they wanted.

722
01:10:14.000 --> 01:10:25.304
So I don't know if that protruded over into this area or not because I wasn't here; I don't know.

723
01:10:25.304 --> 01:10:32.652
But like I said we didn't face much of that because I mean,

724
01:10:32.652 --> 01:10:44.522
we knew the kids went from Vinton to Westlake they had to go when they was passing all these high schools.

725
01:10:44.522 --> 01:10:56.957
But then we got a high school of our own because my daddy pushed and he pushed for Mossville to have everything.

726
01:10:56.957 --> 01:11:00.348
We even had a post office.

727
01:11:00.348 --> 01:11:02.609
And we had everything.

728
01:11:02.609 --> 01:11:11.652
Mossville was one of the best places . . . Best cities you could have been brought up in.

729
01:11:11.652 --> 01:11:12.783
A community.

730
01:11:12.783 --> 01:11:15.609
That's all I could say.

731
01:11:15.609 --> 01:11:16.739
Bottom line.

732
01:11:16.739 --> 01:11:19.000
Mossville was good.

733
01:11:19.000 --> 01:11:25.930
<v COOPER>Do you how your father's mother and father met and how they came to be married?

734
01:11:25.930 --> 01:11:27.662
<v DOTSON>No. That was before.

735
01:11:27.662 --> 01:11:33.726
I never even thought about that kind of stuff when I was growing up.

736
01:11:33.726 --> 01:11:37.191
Well she was dead when I was born.

737
01:11:37.191 --> 01:11:50.185
But I know Grandma [Linney?] used to live . . . They had a house in the front yard is what my mom told me because she used to live right by us.

738
01:11:50.185 --> 01:11:53.217
Now my sister remembered her very well,

739
01:11:53.217 --> 01:12:10.975
but I don't even know if my sister knew who my grandmother's people were and if they were . . . They just didn't talk about that kind of stuff too much during that time and then I was too young to be nosey,

740
01:12:10.975 --> 01:12:14.439
to try to find out who was who.

741
01:12:14.439 --> 01:12:17.038
I was just happy being me.

742
01:12:17.038 --> 01:12:20.070
We was too busy being a kid.

743
01:12:20.070 --> 01:12:27.000
And because my daddy died when I was seven and . . . But I remember a lot.

744
01:12:27.000 --> 01:12:31.139
<v COOPER>Some people mention the importance of voting.

745
01:12:31.139 --> 01:12:32.912
Especially after the

746
01:12:32.912 --> 01:12:35.869
<c.p>Voting Rights Act of 1965.

747
01:12:35.869 --> 01:12:41.190
And you were about seventeen or eighteen right then.

748
01:12:41.190 --> 01:12:52.423
Do you have any memories of voting for the first time or what that was like in the community?

749
01:12:52.423 --> 01:13:00.109
<v DOTSON>No I can't remember. [Phone rings] This phone don't usually ring this much.

750
01:13:00.109 --> 01:13:01.883
That's my son.

751
01:13:01.883 --> 01:13:02.474
Hello?

752
01:13:02.474 --> 01:13:03.066
Hey.

753
01:13:03.066 --> 01:13:04.839
You didn't call.

754
01:13:04.839 --> 01:13:07.204
I didn't see it.

755
01:13:07.204 --> 01:13:10.161
Y'all on y'all way back?

756
01:13:10.161 --> 01:13:10.752
Okay.

757
01:13:10.752 --> 01:13:13.708
People over her interviewing me.

758
01:13:13.708 --> 01:13:16.664
From Mossville . . . About the Mossville.

759
01:13:16.664 --> 01:13:21.394
Okay so I'm going to call you back.

760
01:13:21.394 --> 01:13:21.985
Okay.

761
01:13:21.985 --> 01:13:22.577
Bye.

762
01:13:22.577 --> 01:13:24.350
That's my son.

763
01:13:24.350 --> 01:13:27.898
He's on vacation there in Florida.

764
01:13:27.898 --> 01:13:32.628
Wanted to know why didn't I call him.

765
01:13:32.628 --> 01:13:33.219
<v COOPER>Okay.

766
01:13:33.219 --> 01:13:33.810
Perfect.

767
01:13:33.810 --> 01:13:34.401
<v DOTSON>Okay.

768
01:13:34.401 --> 01:13:48.000
<v COOPER>So you . . . I was just asking you about the Voting Rights Act and if you remembered any of that in the community?

769
01:13:48.000 --> 01:13:57.899
<v DOTSON>No. My daddy was . . . my daddy . . . I know my daddy back in the day campaigned a lot.

770
01:13:57.899 --> 01:14:01.392
Well, he campaigned for Henry Reed.

771
01:14:01.392 --> 01:14:03.722
And then Jake Rigmaiden.

772
01:14:03.722 --> 01:14:06.051
That was his nephew.

773
01:14:06.051 --> 01:14:11.291
He was politically strong in Mossville too, Audrey Prater.

774
01:14:11.291 --> 01:14:20.608
So they really . . . During that time we didn't really pay attention to many things like that,

775
01:14:20.608 --> 01:14:25.848
but I know Mossville had their own voting precinct.

776
01:14:25.848 --> 01:14:29.924
I don't know what year it started.

777
01:14:29.924 --> 01:14:44.481
But I know Mossville had their own voting precinct and it used to be at the school, then they moved it to the Recreation Center.

778
01:14:44.481 --> 01:14:56.127
So now I'm almost sure that I started voting at Mossville School because they had a precinct there, and I

779
01:14:56.127 --> 01:14:58.456
<c.p>know my mom voted.

780
01:14:58.456 --> 01:15:00.785
All of us voted.

781
01:15:00.785 --> 01:15:04.861
When you got . . . That was a privilege.

782
01:15:04.861 --> 01:15:16.506
You got the age to vote and you get your driving license at fifteen and we got all of that.

783
01:15:16.506 --> 01:15:27.570
I mean we did all that but we don't . . . Like I said, living in Mossville you had it all.

784
01:15:27.570 --> 01:15:32.810
You didn't think of the outside world too much.

785
01:15:32.810 --> 01:15:34.557
You really didn't.

786
01:15:34.557 --> 01:15:51.443
But it would come . . . it was coming to us so . . . but we didn't really think too . . . we were just like a big . . . We just thought we had it all.

787
01:15:51.443 --> 01:15:57.848
That's just the bottom line, we thought we had it all.

788
01:15:57.848 --> 01:16:10.658
And that . . . Now when I got married and I moved to Moss Bluff [Louisiana] . . . I’ve been up here now about forty-four years.

789
01:16:10.658 --> 01:16:12.405
About forty-four years.

790
01:16:12.405 --> 01:16:17.063
But I go back and forth to Mossville.

791
01:16:17.063 --> 01:16:18.810
Mossville was home.

792
01:16:18.810 --> 01:16:25.215
And my children went to school up here in Moss Bluff.

793
01:16:25.215 --> 01:16:30.456
Now my daughter Deidra . . . It was kind of funny.

794
01:16:30.456 --> 01:16:33.949
She went to Moss Bluff Elementary.

795
01:16:33.949 --> 01:16:44.430
She started . . . well we had her at a daycare and my . . . When she went to Moss Bluff Elementary,

796
01:16:44.430 --> 01:16:52.000
out of a hundred and twenty-five kids she was the only black.

797
01:16:52.000 --> 01:16:52.667
<v COOPER>Wow.

798
01:16:52.667 --> 01:17:03.333
<v DOTSON>She was the only black out of a hundred and twenty-five kids at Moss Bluff Elementary.

799
01:17:03.333 --> 01:17:07.333
<v COOPER>And what year was she there?

800
01:17:07.333 --> 01:17:08.667
<v DOTSON>Oh Lord.

801
01:17:08.667 --> 01:17:15.333
I can't remember what year Deidra was at Moss Bluff.

802
01:17:15.333 --> 01:17:21.333
Goodness she was born sixty . . . six, seven, eight, sixty-nine.

803
01:17:21.333 --> 01:17:25.333
Might have been about seventy . . . Seventy-one.

804
01:17:25.333 --> 01:17:28.000
Somewhere along in there.

805
01:17:28.000 --> 01:17:34.000
<v COOPER>And what year were you at Mossville Elementary?

806
01:17:34.000 --> 01:17:37.000
<v DOTSON>Now Deidra was at Moss Bluff.

807
01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:40.500
<v COOPER>Oh she was at Moss Bluff Elementary.

808
01:17:40.500 --> 01:17:41.500
I see.

809
01:17:41.500 --> 01:17:43.000
<v DOTSON>And that's here.

810
01:17:43.000 --> 01:17:43.500
<v COOPER>Right.

811
01:17:43.500 --> 01:17:45.500
<v DOTSON>This is Moss Bluff.

812
01:17:45.500 --> 01:17:49.000
And Mossville is what we're talking about.

813
01:17:49.000 --> 01:17:50.000
<v COOPER>Right.

814
01:17:50.000 --> 01:17:55.217
<v DOTSON>But to try to instill things in my children.

815
01:17:55.217 --> 01:18:02.752
My daughter would come home and say, "Mama they call me a nigger."

816
01:18:02.752 --> 01:18:09.707
I said, "Well baby we’re going to have to deal with that."

817
01:18:09.707 --> 01:18:14.924
And I told her that they were no better.

818
01:18:14.924 --> 01:18:18.981
"These kids are no better than you."

819
01:18:18.981 --> 01:18:24.197
And she couldn’t understand, because we didn't teach that.

820
01:18:24.197 --> 01:18:28.255
And you see, I wasn’t taught that.

821
01:18:28.255 --> 01:18:31.732
And she would come home crying.

822
01:18:31.732 --> 01:18:38.108
So we kept dealing with her until those kids accepted her,

823
01:18:38.108 --> 01:18:50.860
because after a point they didn't look at Deidra as being black because she . . . After a while she'd come home and say,

824
01:18:50.860 --> 01:18:57.815
"I got a boyfriend and his name . . ." What that boy name was?

825
01:18:57.815 --> 01:18:59.554
Brent, I believe.

826
01:18:59.554 --> 01:19:08.828
And she got along well with them, because we tried not to teach her to hate.

827
01:19:08.828 --> 01:19:12.885
These kids’ parents had told them that.

828
01:19:12.885 --> 01:19:21.000
So we were trying to keep our children as colorblind as we could.

829
01:19:21.000 --> 01:19:33.039
<c.p>So Deidra turned out . . . Well when she'd come home she would tell me, "Mama I got to wear my hair hanging long."

830
01:19:33.039 --> 01:19:38.511
Because all of the white kids had their hair down.

831
01:19:38.511 --> 01:19:45.625
So I had to be sure that Deidra’s hair was hanging down too.

832
01:19:45.625 --> 01:19:51.098
Because she used to say, "Plaits make you look stupid."

833
01:19:51.098 --> 01:19:54.928
So she didn't want her hair braided.

834
01:19:54.928 --> 01:20:05.326
So my kids really had it harder with integration than I did because I was almost shield from it.

835
01:20:05.326 --> 01:20:08.609
But my kids . . . It was there.

836
01:20:08.609 --> 01:20:12.440
It was no shielding them from it.

837
01:20:12.440 --> 01:20:15.176
You just couldn't do it.

838
01:20:15.176 --> 01:20:20.648
And what we did . . . Deidra would . . . We laughed about it.

839
01:20:20.648 --> 01:20:25.573
She would come home speaking like she was white.

840
01:20:25.573 --> 01:20:28.857
She was white all day long.

841
01:20:28.857 --> 01:20:35.423
And she was with all of these white kids all day long,

842
01:20:35.423 --> 01:20:44.179
but she at the end of the day she had to come home and be black.

843
01:20:44.179 --> 01:20:51.293
So this was a hard transition for them to make at this time.

844
01:20:51.293 --> 01:20:56.765
But we struggled through it, and Deidra finished high school.

845
01:20:56.765 --> 01:21:09.899
Now they were very prejudiced at Sam Houston during that time because you see she went from Moss Bluff Elementary to Moss Bluff Middle,

846
01:21:09.899 --> 01:21:14.277
which by then was more black kids then.

847
01:21:14.277 --> 01:21:25.769
But a lot of the black people didn't want their children because they think they wasn't going to be treated right.

848
01:21:25.769 --> 01:21:29.052
But Deidra was so strong willed.

849
01:21:29.052 --> 01:21:37.261
She went to a . . . She went to school at Sam Houston and he told us.

850
01:21:37.261 --> 01:21:43.280
She say, "Mama I'm not going to stay there for long."

851
01:21:43.280 --> 01:21:55.866
She said . . . my daughter wanted to . . . She took correspondence courses at that time you didn't have to be failing to take correspondence courses.

852
01:21:55.866 --> 01:22:06.811
And she took correspondence courses from LSU [Louisiana State University] to advance herself to get out of high school faster.

853
01:22:06.811 --> 01:22:13.660
Well in her . . . And then she also went to summer school.

854
01:22:13.660 --> 01:22:24.726
She went to summer school . . . She wanted us to let her go to summer school at Washington High School.

855
01:22:24.726 --> 01:22:45.112
Well we knew our daughter had never been to school with black kids and we wanted her to know what being black was like because she wasn't brought up with a lot of black children.

856
01:22:45.112 --> 01:22:48.025
So we sent her to

857
01:22:48.025 --> 01:22:53.267
<c.p>Washington so she could see how black people lived.

858
01:22:53.267 --> 01:22:59.674
So she wouldn't be just thrown to one side of it.

859
01:22:59.674 --> 01:23:02.586
So she learned to mingle.

860
01:23:02.586 --> 01:23:05.498
She can deal with anybody.

861
01:23:05.498 --> 01:23:16.565
She can . . . She even got to the point where she could deal with a person calling her a nigger.

862
01:23:16.565 --> 01:23:35.204
Because she was brought up white all day and black at night and we sent her to summer school for two years at Washington to try to help her not be mean,

863
01:23:35.204 --> 01:23:40.446
not be angry, to accept things as they were.

864
01:23:40.446 --> 01:23:44.523
And to make a life for yourself.

865
01:23:44.523 --> 01:23:51.512
Deidra turned . . . Deidra was . . . Deidra graduated . . . Her birthday is May the first.

866
01:23:51.512 --> 01:24:05.491
And she graduated from Sam Houston in three years because she said she was getting out of that school because it just wasn't right.

867
01:24:05.491 --> 01:24:11.316
They . . . Moss Bluff was a hard place to live in.

868
01:24:11.316 --> 01:24:17.723
It really was but our house . . . My father-in-law was a plumber.

869
01:24:17.723 --> 01:24:20.635
He always had a lot.

870
01:24:20.635 --> 01:24:32.284
And I guess I'm getting off the subject from Moss Bluff to Mossville but it's all intertwining with my family.

871
01:24:32.284 --> 01:24:41.021
And they burned a cross in his yard so to . . . You see what I'm saying?

872
01:24:41.021 --> 01:24:43.933
I came from Mossville now.

873
01:24:43.933 --> 01:24:51.505
When none of this stuff existed to Moss Bluff where it was prevalent.

874
01:24:51.505 --> 01:24:55.000
It was just wide open.

875
01:24:55.000 --> 01:24:57.965
Now our house was burned down.

876
01:24:57.965 --> 01:25:02.907
And it was a lot of black houses burned down.

877
01:25:02.907 --> 01:25:09.331
And they couldn't prove who did it but they knew it was arson.

878
01:25:09.331 --> 01:25:13.285
And the Klu Klux Klan was bad here.

879
01:25:13.285 --> 01:25:14.767
They were bad.

880
01:25:14.767 --> 01:25:19.709
It was coming from out of Gillis and [Topsie?] area.

881
01:25:19.709 --> 01:25:21.686
It was really bad.

882
01:25:21.686 --> 01:25:25.640
They didn't want to see you with anything.

883
01:25:25.640 --> 01:25:32.064
And our house burned down and my husband said, "We're not going anywhere.

884
01:25:32.064 --> 01:25:39.971
We build right back soon as our insurance . . ." And I think . . . Well we had it insured.

885
01:25:39.971 --> 01:25:42.936
My husband had a good job.

886
01:25:42.936 --> 01:25:43.924
We rebuild.

887
01:25:43.924 --> 01:25:53.314
And I think the FBI came in on it because it was so much being done during that time.

888
01:25:53.314 --> 01:26:05.174
And we had State Farm Insurance and State Farm pushed it because so many of their buildings, their insurance clients, were being burned out.

889
01:26:05.174 --> 01:26:16.541
And I think they pushed it so hard till this did away with a lot of the Klan activities from what had happened.

890
01:26:16.541 --> 01:26:20.000
<v COOPER>Around what time was this happening?

891
01:26:20.000 --> 01:26:22.837
<v DOTSON>That was in the seventies.

892
01:26:22.837 --> 01:26:33.048
But Moss Bluff was rough when you’re talking about integration, it wasn't in Mossville, Moss Bluff was bad.

893
01:26:33.048 --> 01:26:35.885
Lake Charles was bad, too.

894
01:26:35.885 --> 01:26:40.990
But Moss Bluff was were nobody wanted to come.

895
01:26:40.990 --> 01:26:49.500
But we stayed here because my husband was born and raised here in Moss Bluff.

896
01:26:49.500 --> 01:26:51.769
Moss Bluff was bad.

897
01:26:51.769 --> 01:26:56.308
And then I was a school bus driver.

898
01:26:56.308 --> 01:26:58.010
I just retired.

899
01:26:58.010 --> 01:27:05.385
And the first bus that they integrated, it was only two black drivers.

900
01:27:05.385 --> 01:27:12.192
It was on the first bus that they integrated was my bus.

901
01:27:12.192 --> 01:27:12.760
<v COOPER>Wow.

902
01:27:12.760 --> 01:27:16.163
<v DOTSON>And you talk about had problems.

903
01:27:16.163 --> 01:27:19.000
<v COOPER>What year was that?

904
01:27:19.000 --> 01:27:22.368
<v DOTSON>Maybe the eighties, somewhere along in there.

905
01:27:22.368 --> 01:27:34.876
And they . . . when I was . . . When my bus was integrated it was like in trailer parks and they gave me a rough bunch of black kids,

906
01:27:34.876 --> 01:27:39.205
and I had these rough bunch of trailer kids.

907
01:27:39.205 --> 01:27:47.865
And they had mixed . . . The families weren't mixed, but like being brought up by mamas no daddies stuff.

908
01:27:47.865 --> 01:27:54.119
This is where the problems were but thank God I made it through.

909
01:27:54.119 --> 01:27:59.411
I got to be a bus driver that had no color.

910
01:27:59.411 --> 01:28:00.854
I wasn't white.

911
01:28:00.854 --> 01:28:02.297
I wasn't black.

912
01:28:02.297 --> 01:28:05.184
I was just the bus driver.

913
01:28:05.184 --> 01:28:09.995
And thank God with the principal out here, Mr. [Barket?],

914
01:28:09.995 --> 01:28:19.135
I made it through because it was complaints on me in the white community that I was too harsh.

915
01:28:19.135 --> 01:28:23.465
I made the kids sit together, which I did.

916
01:28:23.465 --> 01:28:27.314
You didn't act right you didn't separate yourself.

917
01:28:27.314 --> 01:28:28.276
It wasn’t

918
01:28:28.276 --> 01:28:31.643
<c.p>no white side and no black side.

919
01:28:31.643 --> 01:28:35.973
You don't want to do it we don't move.

920
01:28:35.973 --> 01:28:44.151
We sit right here until you decide you going to sit where I told you to sit.

921
01:28:44.151 --> 01:28:48.000
And that kind of smoothed itself off.

922
01:28:48.000 --> 01:28:53.916
And right down the street here when you go back out you'll see there’s some apartments.

923
01:28:53.916 --> 01:29:01.311
And it used to be a lot of houses in there and it was section eight [Housing Act of 1937].

924
01:29:01.311 --> 01:29:03.529
You know what section eight is?

925
01:29:03.529 --> 01:29:03.899
<v COOPER>[Agrees].

926
01:29:03.899 --> 01:29:09.076
<v DOTSON>And there was a white family in there and I can't remember the names.

927
01:29:09.076 --> 01:29:14.992
And they couldn't find anywhere else to stay so they had to stay there, down there.

928
01:29:14.992 --> 01:29:23.126
And they had two little boys, two little white boys, and these little boys they knew I didn't pick up going down.

929
01:29:23.126 --> 01:29:28.672
I would turn around and pick up coming back and the kids would be playing.

930
01:29:28.672 --> 01:29:38.286
And these two little white boys when the bus got there they didn't want to run and play with them they just stood at the stop.

931
01:29:38.286 --> 01:29:43.832
And they hollered at them, "Come on you bunch of niggers the bus is here!"

932
01:29:43.832 --> 01:29:45.681
Two little white boys now.

933
01:29:45.681 --> 01:29:48.639
"Come on you niggers the bus is coming!

934
01:29:48.639 --> 01:29:50.118
The bus is here!"

935
01:29:50.118 --> 01:29:53.815
And I say, "Baby, do you know what you're saying?"

936
01:29:53.815 --> 01:29:54.924
They didn't know.

937
01:29:54.924 --> 01:30:00.101
They said they daddy said everybody in that trailer . . . In that apartment was niggers.

938
01:30:00.101 --> 01:30:00.840
So okay.

939
01:30:00.840 --> 01:30:04.908
I mean it was funny to me, but it wasn't funny.

940
01:30:04.908 --> 01:30:16.000
Because if these kids had any sense with all these black kids they ought to know they was going . . . They should have got whipped to death in their neighborhood.

941
01:30:16.000 --> 01:30:25.142
So finally I would let them sit in the front because I was protecting these two little white kids.

942
01:30:25.142 --> 01:30:29.472
So these . . . The kids got back on the bus.

943
01:30:29.472 --> 01:30:35.246
I forgot how long, about . . . Maybe they stayed there about a year.

944
01:30:35.246 --> 01:30:41.501
And then one evening they told me they say, "Ms. Dotson, guess what?"

945
01:30:41.501 --> 01:30:44.388
That’s these two little white boys.

946
01:30:44.388 --> 01:30:46.313
I say, "What baby?"

947
01:30:46.313 --> 01:30:49.681
They say, "My daddy said we are

948
01:30:49.681 --> 01:30:51.125
<c.p>going to move."

949
01:30:51.125 --> 01:30:54.012
I said, "Where you moving to?"

950
01:30:54.012 --> 01:31:00.267
He say, "My daddy say we’re going to move where there's no niggers."

951
01:31:00.267 --> 01:31:14.701
I say, "Baby when you go home tell you your daddy that Ms. Dotson said that he could move to the backside of hell and there's going to be niggers.

952
01:31:14.701 --> 01:31:22.881
So I don't know where y'all going." [laughs] So I didn't see them anymore because they moved.

953
01:31:22.881 --> 01:31:37.316
But see they didn't have any sense to think it was hurtful to say that and I didn't take it as though it was hurtful for them to say that,

954
01:31:37.316 --> 01:31:40.684
because I didn't start nothing with it.

955
01:31:40.684 --> 01:31:45.014
And my kids on my bus didn’t start anything.

956
01:31:45.014 --> 01:31:51.751
So you see if you . . . These kids was really taught that by their parents.

957
01:31:51.751 --> 01:32:03.780
But those kids were lucky to get out of there alive if those kids would have been mean in that area where they were living.

958
01:32:03.780 --> 01:32:10.516
So this is just to show you and to say what things were like.

959
01:32:10.516 --> 01:32:12.441
Because I'm sure you,

960
01:32:12.441 --> 01:32:24.470
y'all don't know a lot of . . . Didn't go through a lot of this harsh stuff either because of the areas that you came out of.

961
01:32:24.470 --> 01:32:29.762
But Moss Bluff was a heck of a place to live.

962
01:32:29.762 --> 01:32:32.168
But it is great now.

963
01:32:32.168 --> 01:32:38.904
All of . . . Well it's still some here that you never going to get right.

964
01:32:38.904 --> 01:32:41.791
But the majority of them are.

965
01:32:41.791 --> 01:32:50.452
Because I've driven a school bus in every area in Moss Bluff and I retired at forty-two years.

966
01:32:50.452 --> 01:33:02.000
And to be a black bus driver in all the white areas that I drove in you could tell Moss Bluff had changed.

967
01:33:02.000 --> 01:33:05.130
<v COOPER>Now your mother was a bus driver too.

968
01:33:05.130 --> 01:33:08.261
So how do you think your experiences differed?

969
01:33:08.261 --> 01:33:11.000
<v DOTSON>Well my mom just hauled black kids.

970
01:33:11.000 --> 01:33:16.087
I hauled black kids for a while and then my bus was integrated.

971
01:33:16.087 --> 01:33:18.043
<v COOPER>And she was in Mossville?

972
01:33:18.043 --> 01:33:20.000
She was driving in Mossville?

973
01:33:20.000 --> 01:33:20.391
<v DOTSON>[Agrees].

974
01:33:20.391 --> 01:33:21.957
She was only Mossville.

975
01:33:21.957 --> 01:33:23.130
And Houston River.

976
01:33:23.130 --> 01:33:29.000
Kids from Houston River couldn't go to Westlake they had to come to Mossville.

977
01:33:29.000 --> 01:33:29.478
<v COOPER>Wow.

978
01:33:29.478 --> 01:33:38.081
So you were pretty . . . You were a young adult when Martin Luther King was on the national stage.

979
01:33:38.081 --> 01:33:45.250
Do you remember anything about him or about President Kennedy and Johnson during that time?

980
01:33:45.250 --> 01:33:53.375
<v DOTSON>Yeah I remember when President Kennedy got killed and I remember when Martin Luther King got killed.

981
01:33:53.375 --> 01:33:55.287
That was very hurtful.

982
01:33:55.287 --> 01:34:09.147
It was like our world was . . . We were going to be set back after they died because it was all . . . Everything was progress you know to a certain point.

983
01:34:09.147 --> 01:34:16.316
And it was really . . . It was a sad time in our country during that time.

984
01:34:16.316 --> 01:34:21.096
But like I said we just overcame so many challenges.

985
01:34:21.096 --> 01:34:28.265
Because at the hospital when you go to the hospital it was a colored section.

986
01:34:28.265 --> 01:34:30.176
<v COOPER>The hospital in Mossville?

987
01:34:30.176 --> 01:34:32.088
<v DOTSON>No, in Lake Charles.

988
01:34:32.088 --> 01:34:34.000
<v COOPER>In Lake Charles.

989
01:34:34.000 --> 01:34:37.025
<v DOTSON>Yeah, it was a colored section.

990
01:34:37.025 --> 01:34:44.588
But you were . . . I guess coming out of Mossville and being treated like we were,

991
01:34:44.588 --> 01:34:55.176
and what we had, and we knew, and what our parents was teaching us, just helped us to be better people.

992
01:34:55.176 --> 01:34:59.210
Just helped us to be much better people.

993
01:34:59.210 --> 01:34:59.714
And

994
01:34:59.714 --> 01:35:11.311
<c.p>that's why I said I just thank God that I was born, raised, educated in Mossville and had the parents that I had,

995
01:35:11.311 --> 01:35:19.882
because no telling what I would of been if I hadn't come through where I came from.

996
01:35:19.882 --> 01:35:21.899
I'm proud of Mossville.

997
01:35:21.899 --> 01:35:24.420
I'm really proud of it.

998
01:35:24.420 --> 01:35:34.000
And I hate to see it go away, but Mossville now to me is like alpha and omega.

999
01:35:34.000 --> 01:35:45.007
In the beginning, I was there far as like Mossville has been bought out three times by the plants.

1000
01:35:45.007 --> 01:35:54.276
First time it was by Conoco and they bought out a church and a few families.

1001
01:35:54.276 --> 01:35:58.331
And they were right next to Conoco.

1002
01:35:58.331 --> 01:36:02.966
And then it came to . . . was is Vista?

1003
01:36:02.966 --> 01:36:12.814
It was Vista bought out Bel Air, and that was the second time Mossville was bought out.

1004
01:36:12.814 --> 01:36:15.131
Third time is Sasol.

1005
01:36:15.131 --> 01:36:18.607
And Sasol just wiped us out.

1006
01:36:18.607 --> 01:36:29.614
And that's what I'm saying it was like alpha and the buying out of Mossville by Sasol was omega.

1007
01:36:29.614 --> 01:36:35.407
So it was the beginning and the end of Mossville.

1008
01:36:35.407 --> 01:36:43.517
So that's why I compared it to like in the Bible, alpha and omega.

1009
01:36:43.517 --> 01:36:58.579
So the end of Mossville is really . . . it's really . . . Sasol has . . . With Mossville being bought out it was hurtful because we had so many memories there.

1010
01:36:58.579 --> 01:37:17.117
But so many of the people that were there when Sasol started buying out put them in a better position than they were before because they're able to get a better place,

1011
01:37:17.117 --> 01:37:22.910
they're able to have more to leave to their children.

1012
01:37:22.910 --> 01:37:33.917
Because when Conoco bought out . . . No [when] Vista bought out they didn't give people enough money to even move.

1013
01:37:33.917 --> 01:37:48.400
And these people had to move and incur a lot of debt on trying to move and God knows they weren't able to do it.

1014
01:37:48.400 --> 01:37:55.931
So like I can say Sasol has been a blessing in a way.

1015
01:37:55.931 --> 01:38:09.255
It has been a defeat to what we know but it has been a blessing to a whole lot of people in Mossville.

1016
01:38:09.255 --> 01:38:22.000
I hate to see it go because it was such a great place, but Mossville had gone from greatness to drugs.

1017
01:38:22.000 --> 01:38:28.212
<v COOPER>In the time that Mossville . . . you explained it alpha to omega, the time

1018
01:38:28.212 --> 01:38:39.204
<c.p>that Mossville was a booming community in the time that you were there, how have you seen it change physically over the years?

1019
01:38:39.204 --> 01:38:48.283
<v DOTSON>Mossville had gone from being a place that you would be glad to call . . . Say that you were from,

1020
01:38:48.283 --> 01:39:01.186
to a place that drugs were so prevalent there up until now I would say drugs is still in Mossville but it's not that many people there.

1021
01:39:01.186 --> 01:39:13.133
Gone to a place that you wouldn't want to move into because drugs had gotten so bad and people would say, "Oh you from Mossville?

1022
01:39:13.133 --> 01:39:14.566
Oh my God."

1023
01:39:14.566 --> 01:39:20.779
But you just didn't know what Mossville was like when we were there.

1024
01:39:20.779 --> 01:39:24.124
But people would say, "Oh my God.

1025
01:39:24.124 --> 01:39:25.558
You're from Mossville?"

1026
01:39:25.558 --> 01:39:30.814
Because it had just gone to . . . I wouldn't say the gutter,

1027
01:39:30.814 --> 01:39:37.027
but it has gone done so very much from when I was there.

1028
01:39:37.027 --> 01:39:44.195
<v COOPER>Let’s see, I have so many other categories I want to ask you about too.

1029
01:39:44.195 --> 01:40:03.310
But I think I'm curious right now to know a little more about your mom's . . . the way she was . . . She had plants and doing all of this you know the way you explained with her car was like a truck.

1030
01:40:03.310 --> 01:40:09.044
Do you know if she grew any plants for home remedies or . . .?

1031
01:40:09.044 --> 01:40:10.000
No?

1032
01:40:10.000 --> 01:40:10.541
<v DOTSON>[Disagrees].

1033
01:40:10.541 --> 01:40:12.164
No she didn't.

1034
01:40:12.164 --> 01:40:15.410
She didn't grow anything like that.

1035
01:40:15.410 --> 01:40:20.279
There was a man that used to come around,

1036
01:40:20.279 --> 01:40:30.557
they call him the Mamou Man he used to make mamou tea and you could buy it from him.

1037
01:40:30.557 --> 01:40:34.885
And my mama didn't [. . .?] that kind of stuff.

1038
01:40:34.885 --> 01:40:44.082
She didn't do any of that but she raised vegetables like but that was for our use.

1039
01:40:44.082 --> 01:40:55.984
And figs in their fig trees and stuff . . . COOPER: What sort of dishes now bring you back to your childhood in Mossville?

1040
01:40:55.984 --> 01:41:04.098
<v DOTSON>Potato pies, Lord! [laughs] Oh we would crawfish a lot and out of the ditches.

1041
01:41:04.098 --> 01:41:08.426
We didn't . . . Crawfish wasn't like it is now.

1042
01:41:08.426 --> 01:41:14.377
You used to could go in any ditch and catch crawfish.

1043
01:41:14.377 --> 01:41:16.000
<v COOPER>In Mossville?

1044
01:41:16.000 --> 01:41:24.149
<v DOTSON>Yeah, and crawfish would like . . . If it would rain hard crawfish would be on top of the ground.

1045
01:41:24.149 --> 01:41:29.129
And they had these mounds . . . You ever seen a crawfish mound?

1046
01:41:29.129 --> 01:41:37.278
There's a . . . They would build a mound out of the ground and it would be out of dirt.

1047
01:41:37.278 --> 01:41:47.691
You might can find some of them now, but it's a mound that these crawfish would come out of; out of the ground.

1048
01:41:47.691 --> 01:41:55.387
And we would crawfish, we would pick berries, we'd sell them on the side of the road.

1049
01:41:55.387 --> 01:41:59.461
It was . . . But my mom and like mayhaw jelly.

1050
01:41:59.461 --> 01:42:02.178
Now we had mayhaws in Mossville.

1051
01:42:02.178 --> 01:42:04.894
They grew like in wet spots.

1052
01:42:04.894 --> 01:42:06.705
And blackberries making jellies.

1053
01:42:06.705 --> 01:42:08.516
They would make wine.

1054
01:42:08.516 --> 01:42:10.779
My mama didn't make wine.

1055
01:42:10.779 --> 01:42:13.043
My daddy didn't make wine.

1056
01:42:13.043 --> 01:42:18.023
My daddy used to be a trapper out of Cameron [Louisiana].

1057
01:42:18.023 --> 01:42:20.287
He used to trap muskrats.

1058
01:42:20.287 --> 01:42:23.003
And my daddy could do floors.

1059
01:42:23.003 --> 01:42:25.266
He would take . . . Get pine.

1060
01:42:25.266 --> 01:42:29.794
Buy pinewood and varnish . . . Sand it down and varnish it.

1061
01:42:29.794 --> 01:42:35.679
And he could make the most beautiful floors you ever wanted to see.

1062
01:42:35.679 --> 01:42:44.281
Because we had some in our house and when my daddy died my mama covered them up with carpet.

1063
01:42:44.281 --> 01:42:46.092
I was so glad.

1064
01:42:46.092 --> 01:42:50.166
Because they used to use the Johnson case wax.

1065
01:42:50.166 --> 01:43:01.484
You'd have to get on your knees on the floor and take the wax and rub it on the floors and she had a buffer.

1066
01:43:01.484 --> 01:43:06.917
When the wax would dry she would buff it with the buffer.

1067
01:43:06.917 --> 01:43:08.728
I had that buffer.

1068
01:43:08.728 --> 01:43:12.350
I don't know what I did with it.

1069
01:43:12.350 --> 01:43:14.160
Probably threw it away.

1070
01:43:14.160 --> 01:43:25.479
But it had two brushes under it and it would go around and then you put a pad under them and it would shine it.

1071
01:43:25.479 --> 01:43:27.289
But I was glad.

1072
01:43:27.289 --> 01:43:31.817
My mama said she was so tired of them floors.

1073
01:43:31.817 --> 01:43:34.080
Until she put carpet down.

1074
01:43:34.080 --> 01:43:40.418
I was tired of them because I was . . . Had to get on our knees

1075
01:43:40.418 --> 01:43:42.682
<c.p>and put the wax down.

1076
01:43:42.682 --> 01:43:46.304
It was almost like “wax on wax off.”

1077
01:43:46.304 --> 01:43:48.567
You know in that movie?

1078
01:43:48.567 --> 01:43:52.642
You'd go one way then you'd go another way.

1079
01:43:52.642 --> 01:43:54.000
So . . . But . . .

1080
01:43:54.000 --> 01:44:02.866
<v COOPER>And you . . . who was living . . . which tenants were living in the house when you were . . . When your family was renting it out?

1081
01:44:02.866 --> 01:44:04.075
<v DOTSON>Oh my God.

1082
01:44:04.075 --> 01:44:09.313
Almost anybody you interviewed will tell you that they lived in those apartments.

1083
01:44:09.313 --> 01:44:12.134
It was . . . Oh I mean just everybody.

1084
01:44:12.134 --> 01:44:15.358
I mean people that I didn't even remember.

1085
01:44:15.358 --> 01:44:21.000
Because it was one man that I do remember his name was Henry Duncans.

1086
01:44:21.000 --> 01:44:25.030
He lived in Jennings [Louisiana] and he worked at P.P.G.

1087
01:44:25.030 --> 01:44:31.881
They used to call that Columbus Southern, and he lived there I don't know how many years.

1088
01:44:31.881 --> 01:44:36.313
He lived there and he would drive home on the weekends.

1089
01:44:36.313 --> 01:44:37.925
But he lived there.

1090
01:44:37.925 --> 01:44:41.552
And he finally retired and moved back to Jennings.

1091
01:44:41.552 --> 01:44:44.776
But that's the first one I can remember.

1092
01:44:44.776 --> 01:44:46.791
His name was Henry Duncans.

1093
01:44:46.791 --> 01:44:54.448
And then other people, the families . . .I mean I just can't tell you who all lived in that apartment.

1094
01:44:54.448 --> 01:45:02.507
But it was . . . My mama had four units and I just can’t remember all that people that lived in there.

1095
01:45:02.507 --> 01:45:06.940
<v COOPER>So your mother continued to rent after your father passed away?

1096
01:45:06.940 --> 01:45:07.343
<v DOTSON>[Agrees].

1097
01:45:07.343 --> 01:45:07.746
<v COOPER>Wow.

1098
01:45:07.746 --> 01:45:15.000
And do you have any memories of growing up with other tenants living in the house too?

1099
01:45:15.000 --> 01:45:17.903
<v DOTSON>I can remember some of them.

1100
01:45:17.903 --> 01:45:20.323
The Lemelle lived down there.

1101
01:45:20.323 --> 01:45:24.677
They lived . . . They were kin to Butch Lemelle now.

1102
01:45:24.677 --> 01:45:26.129
You know Butch?

1103
01:45:26.129 --> 01:45:28.548
They were kin to Butch.

1104
01:45:28.548 --> 01:45:30.968
And let's see who else.

1105
01:45:30.968 --> 01:45:39.194
Oh I just can't remember who all lived there but it was just so many different families.

1106
01:45:39.194 --> 01:45:44.516
The Williams family I remember them because they had three kids.

1107
01:45:44.516 --> 01:45:48.871
Oh I just don't remember who all stayed there.

1108
01:45:48.871 --> 01:45:53.226
It was just too many of them to remember.

1109
01:45:53.226 --> 01:45:57.581
But my sister probably remember a lot of them.

1110
01:45:57.581 --> 01:46:01.452
But a lot of families lived in there.

1111
01:46:01.452 --> 01:46:05.806
<v COOPER>And where did you buy clothes from growing up?

1112
01:46:05.806 --> 01:46:13.065
<v DOTSON>My mom bought a lot . . . Well all our clothes was bought out of Lake Charles.

1113
01:46:13.065 --> 01:46:19.355
And I can remember in Westlake there was a store, a clothing store,

1114
01:46:19.355 --> 01:46:33.871
I can't remember the name of it and a lot of the things we made because they had a couple ladies that lived in the neighborhood who'd take in sewing.

1115
01:46:33.871 --> 01:46:36.774
And they could sew real good.

1116
01:46:36.774 --> 01:46:41.129
So a lot of our clothes were made too.

1117
01:46:41.129 --> 01:46:45.000
<v COOPER>Do you remember who the seamstresses were?

1118
01:46:45.000 --> 01:46:51.788
<v DOTSON>One of them was Roberta Cotloeng and the other one was Frankie Gasaway.

1119
01:46:51.788 --> 01:46:55.965
They were very . . . Oh and there's another one.

1120
01:46:55.965 --> 01:47:03.796
They called her Ms. Sweetie and she was handicapped but she could sew real good.

1121
01:47:03.796 --> 01:47:07.973
So those are the three that I knew.

1122
01:47:07.973 --> 01:47:15.283
You would buy your pattern and your material and you’d take it to them.

1123
01:47:15.283 --> 01:47:25.204
And I can remember my mom used to buy a lot of things from Rift's store in Lake Charles.

1124
01:47:25.204 --> 01:47:28.858
That was one of the . . . and [Muller's?].

1125
01:47:28.858 --> 01:47:36.168
And she bought a lot of things there because that was the better stores.

1126
01:47:36.168 --> 01:47:38.779
But . . . and the Fair Store

1127
01:47:38.779 --> 01:47:39.823
<c.p>and [LaVene's?].

1128
01:47:39.823 --> 01:47:40.867
No, [Lernna's?].

1129
01:47:40.867 --> 01:47:50.265
And a lot of stuff was bought there for our clothes and so it was a good variety.

1130
01:47:50.265 --> 01:47:57.053
<v COOPER>And were there . . . Was there a local library that you would go to?

1131
01:47:57.053 --> 01:48:04.363
<v DOTSON>The only library that we had was I think it was one in Westlake.

1132
01:48:04.363 --> 01:48:10.628
But the only library . . . Well we had a library in the school.

1133
01:48:10.628 --> 01:48:18.982
So you see when we moved into the high school they had a big library there.

1134
01:48:18.982 --> 01:48:28.381
But we didn't have one, a library as such, that we . . . Because see the school open in fifty-five.

1135
01:48:28.381 --> 01:48:32.035
So that was the library at school.

1136
01:48:32.035 --> 01:48:37.779
But I think it was one in Westlake and probably Sulphur.

1137
01:48:37.779 --> 01:48:43.000
But we probably wasn't able to go to them.

1138
01:48:43.000 --> 01:48:55.131
<v COOPER>Also I wanted to ask you more too about your father helping to get the high school to come to Mossville.

1139
01:48:55.131 --> 01:49:04.375
Do you remember what it . . . Any stories from when he was campaigning to make that happen?

1140
01:49:04.375 --> 01:49:10.729
<v DOTSON>No, he was . . . I can’t even remember, but he pushed hard.

1141
01:49:10.729 --> 01:49:24.594
He talked to all of the important people and I know with the help of Henry Reed they got it done; got the land.

1142
01:49:24.594 --> 01:49:36.147
I think the land was . . . I don't know if they bought it or if it was donated to the school.

1143
01:49:36.147 --> 01:49:43.080
But getting people to donate land was for the improvement of Mossville.

1144
01:49:43.080 --> 01:49:45.968
Yes, they would do it.

1145
01:49:45.968 --> 01:49:54.056
<v COOPER>And did your mom . . . I wanted to ask another question about this cemetery too.

1146
01:49:54.056 --> 01:49:59.255
Did she have family members buried in that cemetery?

1147
01:49:59.255 --> 01:49:59.833
<v DOTSON>Yes.

1148
01:49:59.833 --> 01:50:03.299
Well my daddy was buried there.

1149
01:50:03.299 --> 01:50:06.765
And his sister was buried there.

1150
01:50:06.765 --> 01:50:11.386
My . . . One of my brother-in-laws was buried there,

1151
01:50:11.386 --> 01:50:23.518
and I have a set of twins that's buried there, and Uncle [Wassey?] was buried there. [Shola?] Rigmaiden was buried there.

1152
01:50:23.518 --> 01:50:32.761
See they had like . . . That cemetery was like more Rigmaidens and Hartmans and Praters buried there,

1153
01:50:32.761 --> 01:50:42.004
and Mount Zion Baptist Church there's another cemetery behind Mount Zion was like were the Mosses,

1154
01:50:42.004 --> 01:50:48.359
the Perkins, the Vincents . . . the Baptist people was buried over there.

1155
01:50:48.359 --> 01:50:55.869
And but it's a lot of people that was buried behind the church.

1156
01:50:55.869 --> 01:51:00.490
Behind . . . Well in Morning Star Baptist Church graveyard.

1157
01:51:00.490 --> 01:51:08.000
But there was two predominantly . . . graveyards that was in Moss Bluff . . . Mossville.

1158
01:51:08.000 --> 01:51:17.788
<v COOPER>And what was the name of the church that you all went to . . . Your family went to growing up?

1159
01:51:17.788 --> 01:51:29.121
<v DOTSON>We went to Morning Star, Christ Sanctified Holy Church, and we went to another church which was a Sanctified church in Brownsville.

1160
01:51:29.121 --> 01:51:34.273
It's . . . Brownsville is like . . . It's an area in Lake Charles.

1161
01:51:34.273 --> 01:51:36.848
It's off of Lake Street.

1162
01:51:36.848 --> 01:51:39.424
My daddy built that church.

1163
01:51:39.424 --> 01:51:43.030
My daddy built Morning Star Church, too.

1164
01:51:43.030 --> 01:51:49.727
He built the church in Lake Charles which we went there for years,

1165
01:51:49.727 --> 01:52:00.030
and it was called The Rose of Sharon Christ Sanctified Holy Church and that was in the Lake Charles area.

1166
01:52:00.030 --> 01:52:07.758
And we had one in Mossville and one in Lake Charles that was predominately here.

1167
01:52:07.758 --> 01:52:18.061
But it was like my daddy built churches like in Rayne Louisiana. [Momentah?], [Branch?], Jennings, Liberty, Texas, Indian Hill, Texas.

1168
01:52:18.061 --> 01:52:24.758
Wherever there was a Sanctified church my daddy was instrumental in building it.

1169
01:52:24.758 --> 01:52:28.364
<v COOPER>And he was also a minister too?

1170
01:52:28.364 --> 01:52:29.909
How . . . Oh sorry.

1171
01:52:29.909 --> 01:52:42.273
<v DOTSON>They would send him to an area that needing building a church and he would stay there was the pastor until it was built.

1172
01:52:42.273 --> 01:52:50.000
<v COOPER>And was your mom involved or were women involved in the church as well?

1173
01:52:50.000 --> 01:53:00.178
<v DOTSON>My mom was involved but she wasn't . . . She didn't travel with daddy a lot to all of the churches that he was building,

1174
01:53:00.178 --> 01:53:06.816
but the main headquarters was in Jennings on Cutting Avenue and he built that church.

1175
01:53:06.816 --> 01:53:13.011
And on the side of that church he built . . . They called it a dormitory.

1176
01:53:13.011 --> 01:53:24.517
They built a dormitory on the side of the church where when you went down as a delegate it was you lived on the church property.

1177
01:53:24.517 --> 01:53:32.925
And they had rooms and then downstairs there was a big kitchen and a big area where you'd eat.

1178
01:53:32.925 --> 01:53:44.431
So the Sanctified Religion back in those days was really a big religion and because my daddy built that church . . . Like they had churches in Beaumont,

1179
01:53:44.431 --> 01:53:47.971
Houston, everybody would come to Jennings in June.

1180
01:53:47.971 --> 01:53:55.494
I forgot . . . Well it was different dates in June that they would go to Jennings for convention.

1181
01:53:55.494 --> 01:53:58.592
But my daddy was big in that.

1182
01:53:58.592 --> 01:54:02.132
<v COOPER>How did he get to be so big?

1183
01:54:02.132 --> 01:54:07.000
How did he get to bring that church to Mossville?

1184
01:54:07.000 --> 01:54:19.804
<v DOTSON>You see “Mother L” and my daddy . . . see she was the original founder of the Christ Sanctified Holy Church.

1185
01:54:19.804 --> 01:54:30.587
And my daddy . . . They were together and that's how it got started, right there in Mossville.

1186
01:54:30.587 --> 01:54:33.957
<v COOPER>So it began in Mossville?

1187
01:54:33.957 --> 01:54:35.978
<v DOTSON>Began in Mossville.

1188
01:54:35.978 --> 01:54:36.652
<v COOPER>Wow.

1189
01:54:36.652 --> 01:54:38.000
<v DOTSON>Yes.

1190
01:54:38.000 --> 01:54:38.333
<v COOPER>Wow.

1191
01:54:38.333 --> 01:54:48.000
Well we are . . . this has been . . . Time has been flying by and it's almost been two hours so I think we should probably wrap up for the first interview.

1192
01:54:48.000 --> 01:54:48.333
<v DOTSON>Okay.

1193
01:54:48.333 --> 01:54:51.333
<v COOPER>But yeah I just . . . On behalf of the museum,

1194
01:54:51.333 --> 01:54:57.000
on behalf of the Oral History Center at LSU, we really want to say thank you for . . .

1195
01:54:57.000 --> 01:54:58.333
<v DOTSON>Oh you’re quite welcome.

1196
01:54:58.333 --> 01:54:59.667
<v COOPER>This has been wonderful.

1197
01:54:59.667 --> 01:55:09.000
And we will also figure out a time if that's okay with you to do a follow up interview and ask some more questions because there’s a lot . . .

1198
01:55:09.000 --> 01:55:09.333
<v DOTSON>Okay.

1199
01:55:09.333 --> 01:55:11.333
<v COOPER>You have so many amazing stories.

1200
01:55:11.333 --> 01:55:16.000
<v DOTSON>And you it's . . . You don't really know what you remember until you start talking.

1201
01:55:16.000 --> 01:55:22.333
And sometimes you can't tie it right into it right then but you think about it as you’re talking.

1202
01:55:22.333 --> 01:55:23.000
But . . .

1203
01:55:23.000 --> 01:55:29.828
<v COOPER>Is there anything else on your mind that you want to say before this interview ends?

1204
01:55:29.828 --> 01:55:34.096
<v DOTSON>Now the young man that you're going to see today,

1205
01:55:34.096 --> 01:55:42.631
his daddy would like pick up . . . See we had like if you had stuff you wanted to get rid of,

1206
01:55:42.631 --> 01:55:44.764
he would pick it up.

1207
01:55:44.764 --> 01:55:49.032
So it was like we had everything in that neighborhood.

1208
01:55:49.032 --> 01:55:51.166
This is what I’m saying.

1209
01:55:51.166 --> 01:56:00.127
He picked up all the trash, all the things that you didn’t want and kept . . . It was like keeping Mossville clean.

1210
01:56:00.127 --> 01:56:05.248
And he would salvage what he could from what he picked up.

1211
01:56:05.248 --> 01:56:05.675
So . . .

1212
01:56:05.675 --> 01:56:08.236
<v COOPER>This is Mr. George Braxton’s father?

1213
01:56:08.236 --> 01:56:08.662
Okay.

1214
01:56:08.662 --> 01:56:11.223
<v DOTSON>Yeah and he was Mr. George.

1215
01:56:11.223 --> 01:56:14.210
That was his daddy’s name, Mr. George.

1216
01:56:14.210 --> 01:56:25.306
So you see we had . . . You don’t think about this but he kept Mossville clean and I never thought about it like that but he did,

1217
01:56:25.306 --> 01:56:27.013
he kept Mossville clean.

1218
01:56:27.013 --> 01:56:28.720
So we had everything!

1219
01:56:28.720 --> 01:56:30.000
<v COOPER>That’s amazing.

1220
01:56:30.000 --> 01:56:30.367
<v DOTSON>We had everything!

1221
01:56:30.367 --> 01:56:30.735
We sure did.

1222
01:56:30.735 --> 01:56:31.102
We had everything.

1223
01:56:31.102 --> 01:56:32.694
So that’s why I keep telling you I’m proud to be from Mossville.

1224
01:56:32.694 --> 01:56:33.306
I’m very proud of Mossville.

1225
01:56:33.306 --> 01:56:33.918
<v COOPER>Well this has been amazing.

1226
01:56:33.918 --> 01:56:35.143
Should we . . . You want to turn off the recorder now?

1227
